WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.210
- Greetings. My name is Timothy Snyder.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:17.690
I'm the Levin Professor of
History at Yale University.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:20.380
I am greeting and
welcoming you this evening

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.480
on behalf of the Franke program,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.020
which has enabled me
along with my colleague,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:28.130
Professor Jason Stanley,

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:32.110
to teach a seminar this
semester on mass incarceration

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:35.126
in the Soviet Union and the United States.

00:00:35.126 --> 00:00:37.860
This is the last of these
lectures that I'll be introducing.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.700
So I want to make a special
point of thanking Richard

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.780
and Barbara Franke for their support

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.980
of the humanities in particular at Yale.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.530
This is much appreciated
in general, in particular,

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:50.800
I've been very glad to be
able to teach this class.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.857
So this seminar on mass
incarceration in USSR

00:00:54.857 --> 00:00:58.350
and in the US is getting
towards its middle point.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:02.464
At this point, our students
have spent a couple of weeks

00:01:02.464 --> 00:01:05.184
reading about the origins of the Gulag

00:01:05.184 --> 00:01:07.580
and the functioning of the Gulag.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.160
This week, you who are not our students,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.440
are joining us as we
move on to the subject

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.446
of the experience of the Gulag.

00:01:15.446 --> 00:01:19.759
One of the most important
chroniclers of the Soviet camps

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:22.710
was a Polish artist whose
name was Józef Czapski.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:27.710
Józef Czapski was, until a few years ago,

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.630
someone who was idolized by, I would say,

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:35.160
the best part of the
Polish intelligentsia.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:38.170
Thanks to our speaker this
evening, Eric Karpeles,

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:40.920
Józef Czapski has moved from being someone

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:44.437
who was known to a few
as an outstanding painter

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:47.046
and chronicler of the 20th century,

00:01:47.046 --> 00:01:49.397
to become a figure who can be now

00:01:49.397 --> 00:01:54.397
and is becoming now
much more widely known.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.350
I'm a historian and I've
always understood Jozef Czapski

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:01.250
in terms of his experiences,
but Eric Karpeles,

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.130
who is himself an artist and a translator,

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.680
as well as a writer,
someone who found Czapski

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:12.190
by way of Proust, which is
an extraordinary connection.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:14.600
When Czapski was in a Soviet camp,

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.030
he was allowed to give French lessons

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.301
and he did so in the
form of recapitulating

00:02:20.301 --> 00:02:24.557
remembrance of things passed
by Proust, which is for me,

00:02:24.557 --> 00:02:27.490
one of the most extraordinary
civilizational achievements

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:32.490
imaginable and deep and
profound in all kinds of ways.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.580
But Eric Karpeles came to Czapski

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.382
from a different direction.

00:02:37.382 --> 00:02:39.599
He came to Czapski by way
of Proust and by way of art

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:42.779
and discovered or
rediscovered him as a painter

00:02:42.779 --> 00:02:45.627
and then has contributed, in fact,

00:02:45.627 --> 00:02:47.740
essentially all by himself,

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:50.880
has created a revival of
Józef Czapski in the west

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:55.840
in the English language by
re-translating the lecture notes

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.700
that Czapski gave about
Proust in the Soviet camp,

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.380
by compiling the art into
an extraordinarily beautiful

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.050
volume which I'm going to
bring to class tomorrow,

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:09.420
and by writing an accessible
and, even for for who thought

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:10.970
he thought he knew the life pretty well,

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:15.100
a striking and original
and persuasive biography

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:18.110
of Czapski called Almost Nothing,

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:21.583
which I'm going to do
my duty and hold up now.

00:03:22.608 --> 00:03:25.253
People speak of dog-eared
copies as though it's a cliche,

00:03:25.253 --> 00:03:27.580
but you can see is
actually a dog-eared copy.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.470
So at this point in the
class, we're very welcome,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:34.105
we're very, very happy to be
able to welcome Eric Karpeles.

00:03:34.105 --> 00:03:38.062
We're trying to consider what
it would be like to experience

00:03:38.062 --> 00:03:41.416
and think about and see the
Gulag and Jozef Czapski,

00:03:41.416 --> 00:03:44.260
there could be no better
figure than Jozef Czapski

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.446
for that, but more than that,

00:03:46.446 --> 00:03:49.251
the class needs to reach a point

00:03:49.251 --> 00:03:51.770
where we understand that
the people who experienced

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.660
the Gulag are experiencing it thoughtfully

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.910
by way of other things they bring to it,

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.680
that their lives are not just determined

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.834
by this experience of incarceration.

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:03.440
Jozef Czapski's an example
of someone for whom

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.857
incarceration was very
important for himself,

00:04:05.857 --> 00:04:08.663
but perhaps more so for his
friends and his comrades

00:04:08.663 --> 00:04:11.320
and his colleagues who he
later tried to remember,

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:13.362
but he was above all an artist who

00:04:13.362 --> 00:04:15.850
lived an almost century-long life.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:18.290
So I'm very pleased at
this point in the class,

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:20.664
we're going to be able to
seriously introduce art

00:04:20.664 --> 00:04:22.840
as part of the history or part

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:25.370
of the approach to mass incarceration.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.333
This is a theme and then
approach alongside philosophy

00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:31.640
and history, which I hope
we'll be able to keep going.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.070
So there's no one better to do this

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:35.260
for us than Eric Karpeles, Eric,

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.990
I'm very glad that you
were able to join us.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:39.770
I welcome you on behalf of all the people

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.440
whom who can see you
but whom you can't see,

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.520
and I'm very much looking
forward to your lecture

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.373
and to your presentation. Thank you.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.600
- It's certainly an honor
to see a copy of my book

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:57.700
read by you with those dog-eared pages.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:00.833
It makes a great impression upon me.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.390
I will say that right now,

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:08.390
I need to see how, so Józef
Czapski: Facing the Void.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:18.120
I have chosen the title as a way

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.548
of expressing the context of this class

00:05:23.548 --> 00:05:25.978
and the way in which Czapski can

00:05:25.978 --> 00:05:30.978
interact with the subject at large.

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:38.400
Facing the Void is in one
way of looking at the world,

00:05:42.503 --> 00:05:45.520
the 20th century, which
Czapski lived through.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.341
He lived into his 97th year,

00:05:48.341 --> 00:05:53.341
and also a way of the
internal life of Czapski

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.070
in which he always felt the need

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:03.070
to create as a way of filling the void,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.710
as a way of making comment
on the world around him.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:12.710
Czapski was an enormously large character.

00:06:14.883 --> 00:06:17.580
He will fit very tightly, be shoehorned,

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.660
into this short lecture of mine,

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:24.390
which could actually warrant
an entire semester of study.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:28.490
So without a lot of further
do, I'm just going to jump in.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:31.360
I've been asked by
Professor Snyder to put him

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:36.360
in a context of the world in
which the class takes place,

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:40.550
and also to speak about him as an artist,

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.350
which I feel more comfortable in,

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.003
but I will run you through all of this.

00:06:48.131 --> 00:06:52.460
So we're going to begin
with this is the world

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:57.460
in which Czapski was born in 1896.

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:04.580
It's a map of Central and
Eastern Europe and Russia.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:08.280
And it rightly suggests the very specific

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:12.300
and firm international imperial borders.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.795
But it fails to note that
beyond these borders,

00:07:15.795 --> 00:07:20.795
cultural and societal
norms were transgressed,

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.940
that this was not something
that stopped at a border,

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:29.940
that these societies all
interacted with each other.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:34.880
Czapski's family was
just outside of Minsk,

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.762
where their lands were, and this is what

00:07:37.762 --> 00:07:40.390
today is known as Belarus.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:47.790
the home in which Czapski
grew up, his childhood home.

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.530
Here he is with his parents on the left

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:54.282
and his five sisters and one brother.

00:07:54.282 --> 00:07:56.640
His mother was an Austrian countess,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.382
and his father was a Polish count,

00:07:59.382 --> 00:08:02.870
a Polish count who grew up
speaking French and German

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:07.390
and Russian, but who only
learned Polish at university.

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:11.210
This was a world peopled by
highly cosmopolitan individuals,

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:13.660
citizens of the world rather than people

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:16.780
defined by a single national identity.

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.922
Czapski had Russian, Baltic,
Czech, and German cousins.

00:08:19.922 --> 00:08:22.316
He was born in Prague in 1896

00:08:22.316 --> 00:08:26.580
in one of his mother's family's palaces.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.823
It was a great life of privilege.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.020
It was his mother, the
Austrian countess, however,

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:35.070
who decided that her
children should be raised

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:37.633
as what she'd like to
call her little Poles.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:42.490
When he turned 13, Czapski
and his younger brother

00:08:43.599 --> 00:08:46.100
were shipped off to St. Petersburg
to improve their Russian,

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:49.390
with the expectation of
their time would come to run

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:53.343
the family estate and to
prepare for the baccalaureate.

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:59.360
Five years later, in
1914, the first World War

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.100
was systematically destroying
the old life of Europe,

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.323
slaughtering a generation of young men.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:08.047
Czapski, still in Petersburg,

00:09:08.047 --> 00:09:11.663
and deeply immersed in
Tolstoyan spiritualism,

00:09:17.336 --> 00:09:22.336
he was very young and
he was very vulnerable

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:29.910
and Tolstoy meant a great deal to him.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:34.910
And he wrestled with his feelings
about religion, about God,

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:41.690
but to keep them from being
sent to the front as war fodder,

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:43.660
Czapski's father enrolled his two boys

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.870
in the elite military training school,

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:48.113
the (indistinct) in St. Petersburg.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:51.550
This self portrait, which
was drawn at the time,

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:55.493
was the first of a long
line of self portraits.

00:09:57.061 --> 00:09:59.571
Czapski presented himself
as a military cadet

00:09:59.571 --> 00:10:01.803
and a young man of the world.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:05.140
He was still in Petersburg
when both the February

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:08.610
and October revolutions erupted in 1917,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.943
and he was deeply marked
by the experience.

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:15.290
He returned to Warsaw in 1918 and decided

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:17.020
to pursue a course of study in painting

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:20.223
rather than to continue on
with his training as a pianist.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:24.308
In February of 1919, tensions exploded

00:10:24.308 --> 00:10:26.653
between the newly-minted
Second Republic of Poland

00:10:26.653 --> 00:10:31.270
and Soviet Russia, not
yet the Soviet Union.

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:32.850
Abandoning his course of study at the

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:34.343
Fine Arts Academy in Warsaw,

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:37.750
Czapski volunteered immediately
for military service.

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:39.910
His courage in combat
earned him the Polish

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:42.740
Order of the Virtuti Militari,

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:45.520
the country's highest military decoration.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.340
He received this medal at the same time

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:50.259
as a French officer would
become a friend of his.

00:10:50.259 --> 00:10:53.610
Charles de Gaulle would have been

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:55.120
sent to Poland as an observer.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:57.623
The friendship survived
over the decades to come.

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:02.210
Back from the front, he enrolled at

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.663
the Academy of Fine Arts in Krakow,

00:11:04.663 --> 00:11:06.860
a city where he knew no one.

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:09.320
Czapski would recall, I entered the world

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:12.330
of my new friends where an
intellectual atmosphere,

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:15.160
marked by a carefree
assurance of winning optimism

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:19.163
and essential joy (indistinct),
was totally alien to me.

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.690
Czapski, as you can see in this picture,

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:26.863
grew to enormous heights. He
was six feet, six inches tall.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:30.727
Everyone, however, knew
him as (indistinct),

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:34.783
which is an affectionate
diminutive of the name Jozef.

00:11:35.631 --> 00:11:38.960
It's one of those contradictions
that Czapski's life

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:42.457
was full of, but largely it's the sense

00:11:42.457 --> 00:11:46.423
of the scale of him that's
so impressive, always.

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:55.100
As a painting student he was
caught between the historical

00:11:55.392 --> 00:11:59.745
religious requirements of the
traditional Polish painting,

00:11:59.745 --> 00:12:03.475
and at the same time, what
was going on in Warsaw

00:12:03.475 --> 00:12:07.742
was the fashionable
leaning towards post-cubic,

00:12:07.742 --> 00:12:12.742
Cubist ideological movements
like Futurism and Formism.

00:12:14.010 --> 00:12:16.610
Neither of these was
particularly appealing to him

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:18.840
as a painter, as a painting student,

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:23.090
and he and a group of like-minded
students bonded together.

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:26.600
They raised money hoping
they could finance

00:12:27.463 --> 00:12:29.213
a trip to spend six weeks in Paris,

00:12:30.312 --> 00:12:31.443
where all the action was in painting.

00:12:32.563 --> 00:12:33.473
They felt they were in a backwater.

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.765
They recognized that and hoped to move on.

00:12:36.765 --> 00:12:38.320
They gave a ball to raise money.

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.110
They did all sorts of fundraising things,

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:44.493
and they raised enough money
to spend six weeks in Paris.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.900
They succeeded and departed for Paris,

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:53.900
but instead of staying six
weeks, they stayed seven years.

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:57.080
They became known as
the Committee of Paris

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:58.970
and ever after they returned to Poland,

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:00.493
they were known as the Kapists.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:10.136
Czapski's seven years in Paris
were enormously important.

00:13:10.136 --> 00:13:13.140
They were marked by his immersion

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.190
and the painting of Bonnard, Matisse.

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:17.930
The groups attending professor,

00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:20.210
the painter Jozef Pankiewicz,

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:21.650
who is shown in the self portrait on

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:23.200
the bottom left of the screen,

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.060
was an old friend of Bonnard.

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:27.150
The two of them used to
go to the south together

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:29.193
every summer and paint side by side.

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:35.447
It was a time in which he
shared with his sister,

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:40.650
Maria, who became
enormously important to him.

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:43.210
And the two of them were together

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:46.750
for most of their lives, their long lives.

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:49.410
She was also as tall as
he, she was very striking.

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:50.973
She became an historian.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:55.180
Eventually the Polish
painters had the excitement

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:56.980
of their own tourist exhibition,

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:00.240
which gained them praise
from such recognized figures

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:03.431
as Dufy, Rouault, and
even Bonnard himself.

00:14:03.431 --> 00:14:07.406
Czapski was able to sell
his first paintings,

00:14:07.406 --> 00:14:10.173
including one to Gertrude Stein.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:12.970
On the right, you see the image

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:16.180
of what's called the
(indistinct), which is,

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:20.940
that building was where
the painters communally met

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:23.273
every day to work in studio space.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.320
There were many influences
on Czapski at the time.

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:32.810
He had come to Paris to see
the real painting going on.

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:36.078
In the following groups of
slides, I've shown on the left,

00:14:36.078 --> 00:14:39.770
a known painter in Czapski's work

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:43.843
that somehow shows the
influence and the effect

00:14:43.843 --> 00:14:47.170
of coming in contact with
this kind of painting.

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:51.640
Here's Cezanne. The fellow on the right,

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.840
the portrait is of a
man named (indistinct),

00:14:55.557 --> 00:14:59.440
who was an art critic that Czapski knew.

00:15:00.425 --> 00:15:03.723
Here is a Matisse on the left,

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:06.660
and on the right we see Czapski's

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.059
closest attempt at Fauvism.

00:15:09.059 --> 00:15:12.570
This is a way in which
you see the influence

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:17.570
of technique of color, of form,
learning from the masters.

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:23.093
Soutine, Czapski on the right.

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:30.543
Czapski on the left in this case.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:38.117
You can see him painting
with a certain crude stroke,

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:43.091
and yet with a great animation of vitality

00:15:43.091 --> 00:15:46.293
and of an interest in color.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:52.950
Bonnard was a terrific
influence on Czapski.

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:56.930
These works on paper with
pencil and watercolor

00:15:56.930 --> 00:15:59.850
and colored pencil, but primarily

00:15:59.850 --> 00:16:04.073
in the intensity of the
color in his canvases.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:11.100
In 1930, after having
mounted the group exhibition

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:15.230
of the galleries art, Czapski
headed to Spain for six weeks.

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:16.750
In the vast halls of the Prado,

00:16:16.750 --> 00:16:19.690
he had what he called
the shock of my life.

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:21.280
Standing before the huge array

00:16:22.188 --> 00:16:23.700
of works by Goya, he was overwhelmed.

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:27.501
His masterful portraits of great ladies,

00:16:27.501 --> 00:16:30.310
dignitaries, cardinals and kings,

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:33.010
are painted so smoothly to the degree

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:35.100
that you don't feel the brush.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:37.360
They're classical in
composition and miraculous

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:39.820
in the control of every
detail, Czapski wrote.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:42.330
And in the materiality of the object,

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:44.090
in the consideration given to local

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:46.796
colors and the gradation of values.

00:16:46.796 --> 00:16:50.950
Next to these portraits, in another room,

00:16:54.626 --> 00:16:58.030
hung badly, were paintings by Goya

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:00.760
so extremely different that
it was difficult to believe

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:02.610
they were painted by the same person.

00:17:03.580 --> 00:17:06.840
War scenes, wild scenes
with which is so frenzied.

00:17:06.840 --> 00:17:08.353
We see all the brush strokes.

00:17:09.358 --> 00:17:12.330
Color contrast is violent.
They rival Soutine.

00:17:12.330 --> 00:17:16.000
And the disdain for local
color and the objects

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:19.740
materiality such that the
canvas is sometimes giving

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:23.560
the impression of almost
abstract color relations.

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:25.480
Speaking of myself, Czapski went on,

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:28.660
I was long tormented by
a duality of approach.

00:17:28.660 --> 00:17:30.770
Analytic on the one hand, rational,

00:17:30.770 --> 00:17:32.860
growing from the Dutch tradition.

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:35.062
And on the other hand, the
mad, the unpredictable,

00:17:35.062 --> 00:17:38.480
the true leap into the abyss.

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:41.973
In this, I saw a lack of
integrated personality in myself.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:46.240
A kind of psychic dividedness that I tried

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:49.680
to overcome artificially without success,

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:52.250
but then I noticed the same
phenomenon in the painter

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.576
who's statue was not only
equal to that in the Matisse,

00:17:54.576 --> 00:17:58.463
but who was one of the very
greatest. I speak about Goya.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:07.730
In 1931 the group of Polish
painters returned to Poland.

00:18:11.510 --> 00:18:14.910
Czapski set up a studio in
Warsaw and entered directly

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:17.490
into the flow of the artistic scene there.

00:18:17.490 --> 00:18:19.760
He began to write about painting as well,

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:23.433
and to exhibit his work on a yearly basis.

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:28.580
We find that his work from these years,

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:33.580
the seed of the French style,
transplanted into Polish soil,

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.963
taking root, bearing fruit.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:40.260
These paintings tend to
conform to a range of stylistic

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:43.150
options that feel imposed from without,

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:45.640
tried on like various guises,

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:49.237
rather than emanating from
somewhere within strengthened.

00:18:49.237 --> 00:18:51.178
Strengthened by Goya's example,

00:18:51.178 --> 00:18:52.670
Czapski is nevertheless still finding

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:54.933
his way, learning as he goes.

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:58.623
These paintings were done at a time

00:18:58.623 --> 00:19:02.080
when the Bloomsbury group painters

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:06.440
were actually very closely
experimenting in the same way

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:09.270
with changing old painting styles.

00:19:09.270 --> 00:19:12.453
Here's Czapski coming down the hallway.

00:19:12.453 --> 00:19:17.453
You can see that yellow bar
about a third of the way up

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.630
from the bottom of the
canvas, across the canvas,

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.626
which is not quite opaque in
which you can see the turn

00:19:23.626 --> 00:19:27.230
of the carpet coming
towards you down the hall.

00:19:27.230 --> 00:19:29.330
Czapski is still learning his medium here.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.380
In the mid 1930s, Czapski met a young

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:40.570
writer named Ludwig Hering.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:43.410
In their own tortured way, they overcame

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:45.784
their constraining religious
scruples and allowed themselves

00:19:45.784 --> 00:19:48.814
to fall in love, to set up
home as far as was possible

00:19:48.814 --> 00:19:52.770
from so oppressive an environment.

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:55.170
Knowledge of this unconventional setup

00:19:55.170 --> 00:19:58.097
of what was perceived as their
flaunting of societal norms

00:19:58.097 --> 00:20:01.030
is what has continued to this day to mute

00:20:01.030 --> 00:20:03.730
the response of the community in Poland

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:05.820
to Czapski's achievements and his

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:08.273
indisputable historical significance.

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:12.320
The two men had several
heartening, emotional,

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:15.810
nurturing years together and
remained in close spiritual

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:18.820
contact until the death of Hering, who,

00:20:18.820 --> 00:20:22.723
ill with cancer,
committed suicide in 1984.

00:20:27.404 --> 00:20:29.606
This is one of Czapski's paintings

00:20:29.606 --> 00:20:33.533
just before war broke out, 1937.

00:20:33.533 --> 00:20:38.520
This is the promenade on the Baltic Sea,

00:20:38.520 --> 00:20:41.123
just outside of what was
then known as Danzig.

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:46.803
You can see the Nazi
flags along the promenade.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:55.382
On September 1st, Poland
was invaded by Germany

00:20:55.382 --> 00:20:58.277
in the air, on the ground, the sea.

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:03.610
September 2nd, the next day,

00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:05.840
Czapski went to join his cavalry regiment

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:09.053
as a reserve officer, aged 43.

00:21:10.736 --> 00:21:12.360
On the 27th of September,
he and his regimen

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:14.523
were surrounded by
Germans outside of Lviv.

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:19.020
Surprisingly, they were handed
over to the Russian forces

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:22.510
who had invaded Poland
from the east on the 16th.

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:25.268
He spent six months at
Starobielsk, in Ukraine,

00:21:25.268 --> 00:21:27.921
as a prisoner of war in a
camp with at first 11,000

00:21:27.921 --> 00:21:32.440
fellow prisoners under severe conditions

00:21:32.440 --> 00:21:36.923
of crowding and lack of
food in the first months.

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:41.887
In June of 1940, he
was finally transferred

00:21:41.887 --> 00:21:46.887
with very few other of
his prisoner colleagues

00:21:48.498 --> 00:21:52.413
to a camp at (indistinct),
north of Moscow.

00:21:57.807 --> 00:22:00.430
There's a self portrait he
made in camp (indistinct).

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:03.790
This is the beginning of
our understanding of Czapski

00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:08.500
and his relationship to
drawing and to his journals.

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:11.410
He kept a journal throughout the war,

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:13.540
which became a source
of much of his writing

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:15.953
and of his memory of the experience.

00:22:17.670 --> 00:22:22.670
In the book by Julius
Margolin that you're also

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:29.000
reading for this class, he
says that no one in the camps,

00:22:29.643 --> 00:22:32.990
no one retains his original form.

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:35.240
Observation is difficult because

00:22:35.240 --> 00:22:37.483
the observer himself is deformed.

00:22:39.160 --> 00:22:42.108
This was a test that
Czapski had set himself

00:22:42.108 --> 00:22:46.067
in an attempt not to become deformed,

00:22:46.067 --> 00:22:48.150
to remain true to himself and to help

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:51.223
his fellow prisoners also keep

00:22:51.223 --> 00:22:55.120
focused and keep true to themselves.

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:58.510
They were constantly
bombarded by Soviet propaganda

00:22:58.510 --> 00:23:00.674
over loud speakers in the camp,

00:23:00.674 --> 00:23:04.858
but they struggled to
keep true to themselves.

00:23:04.858 --> 00:23:09.858
Czapski's war was not the
same, certainly, as Margolin's.

00:23:10.506 --> 00:23:15.010
There were different territories
and different demands

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:17.060
upon each of them, but there was something

00:23:17.060 --> 00:23:21.890
about Czapski's resistance
to the hammering of Soviet

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:24.723
propaganda that's very inspiring.

00:23:29.717 --> 00:23:34.717
In Russia, paper was enormously valuable.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:37.945
It was not of good quality,

00:23:37.945 --> 00:23:39.360
but it was very rare and hard to come by.

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:41.190
He did whatever he could to get

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:43.930
notebooks and he filled them.

00:23:43.930 --> 00:23:47.230
This is a drawing of
his friends in the camp,

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:49.400
a man named Adam Shinski.

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:52.980
This is a drawing on both
sides of one piece of paper.

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:56.085
I'm showing both sides of it.
He filled every page he could.

00:23:56.085 --> 00:23:59.568
And got pencils as he could
and materials as he could.

00:23:59.568 --> 00:24:02.771
But painting was out of
the question, really.

00:24:02.771 --> 00:24:07.360
Drawing and use of watercolors was what

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:09.543
he had at hand and he made the best of it.

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:19.603
More drawings of prison companions.

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:24.740
He wrote the sense of being totally unsure

00:24:24.740 --> 00:24:26.950
if we would ever see
anything again in our lives

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:31.950
apart from barbed wire fences
and this alien hostile world.

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:35.510
The sense of being buried alive was cruel.

00:24:35.510 --> 00:24:37.420
It seemed very unlikely that we would ever

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:39.900
get beyond the wire unless to get

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:41.803
a bullet in the back of the head.

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:50.540
Professor Snyder made
reference to the French lessons

00:24:50.540 --> 00:24:52.510
that Czapski was able to give.

00:24:52.510 --> 00:24:56.630
This is his French notebook for
those lectures that he gave.

00:24:56.630 --> 00:25:00.258
He decided to lecture Herisod every night,

00:25:00.258 --> 00:25:02.260
tell a little bit of the portion of

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:07.260
Proust's great novel,
In Search of Lost Time.

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:10.550
He took extensive notes,

00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:13.040
which are in themselves
quite beautiful drawings.

00:25:13.040 --> 00:25:16.250
If you know Proust, you can
see a little bit on the left,

00:25:16.250 --> 00:25:18.400
some of the names of the characters,

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:22.292
but the sketch itself, the
diagram in which he unfolds

00:25:22.292 --> 00:25:26.727
Czapski like a cosmology,

00:25:26.727 --> 00:25:31.727
is in both Polish and French
and German and Russian.

00:25:33.970 --> 00:25:37.113
Different pages carrying
different diagrams.

00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:42.915
This was an understanding for Czapski

00:25:42.915 --> 00:25:47.915
of the attempt to
remember what he had read.

00:25:49.241 --> 00:25:51.260
He read Proust in the
'20s when he was in Paris

00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:53.023
as each of the volumes came out,

00:25:54.345 --> 00:25:55.940
he was in a Soviet camp
where he had no books,

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:57.600
no access to the material.

00:25:57.600 --> 00:25:59.780
So in order to speak about Proust,

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:01.537
he had to remember Proust.

00:26:01.537 --> 00:26:04.300
And this is in a way a
kind of manifestation

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:07.220
of the very Proustian enterprise,

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:09.700
the attempt to recall the past and to make

00:26:09.700 --> 00:26:11.513
use of it in the present.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:16.330
He wrote an intellectual effort conducted

00:26:16.330 --> 00:26:19.130
without books or notes
gives an entirely different

00:26:19.130 --> 00:26:22.960
sensation then when carried
out under normal conditions.

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:26.392
Once involuntary memory
acts much more forcefully,

00:26:26.392 --> 00:26:28.620
the memory of which Proust speaks,

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:29.700
in which he considered to be

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:32.240
the sole source of literary creation.

00:26:32.240 --> 00:26:33.950
After a certain amount of time,

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:36.600
things surface in our consciousnesses.

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.560
Details we haven't the slightest idea

00:26:38.560 --> 00:26:40.911
were even stored anywhere in our brain.

00:26:40.911 --> 00:26:43.950
What is more, these memories that arise

00:26:43.950 --> 00:26:47.420
from our subconscious
are more deeply rooted,

00:26:47.420 --> 00:26:50.763
more intimately bound up
with another, more personal.

00:26:54.020 --> 00:26:57.837
Czapski helps his fellow
prisoners to again

00:26:57.837 --> 00:27:01.160
nurture themselves, their interior lives,

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:05.450
and to use this experience
of sharing Proust

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:10.450
as a way of hardening them
against the realities outside,

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:15.313
and nurturing their inner lives.

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:23.760
On June 22nd, 1941,
everything was upended.

00:27:24.330 --> 00:27:25.670
To this day, Czapski wrote,

00:27:25.670 --> 00:27:27.780
I can still hear ringing in my ears,

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:30.590
a scruffy Colonel bending his
upper body from the window,

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:33.780
shouting, Hitler attack Russia.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:35.610
A new phase of the war began.

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:38.642
Stalin provisionally agreed
to free all Polish prisoners

00:27:38.642 --> 00:27:40.944
and deportees in order
to form an independent

00:27:40.944 --> 00:27:43.402
Polish army within the Red Army to fight

00:27:43.402 --> 00:27:47.913
their former ally, now their common enemy.

00:27:53.210 --> 00:27:55.940
General Wladyslaw Anders,

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:59.207
confined in Moscow's
notorious Lubyanka prison,

00:27:59.207 --> 00:28:03.263
where he had been tortured and
spent 27 months in solitary,

00:28:03.263 --> 00:28:07.033
had been chosen to command
the new Polish order.

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:10.770
He had no shoes. He
walked slowly from wounds

00:28:10.770 --> 00:28:14.153
inflicted by beatings, and
he wore a prison uniform.

00:28:15.280 --> 00:28:17.890
On that day he was introduced
to two of Russia's most

00:28:17.890 --> 00:28:22.170
powerful Politburo members,
(indistinct) and Beria,

00:28:22.170 --> 00:28:24.690
who escorted him to a
limousine where he was whisked

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:26.817
to meetings about the formation of a new

00:28:26.817 --> 00:28:29.533
Polish division of the Red Army.

00:28:31.290 --> 00:28:35.180
Soldier-prisoners began to
gather from all the vast corners

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:38.283
of the Gulag to join
the new Polish division.

00:28:39.500 --> 00:28:44.273
But most of the officers never reappeared.

00:28:46.660 --> 00:28:48.330
Czapski was assigned to interrogate

00:28:48.330 --> 00:28:51.034
the new arrivals to investigate

00:28:51.034 --> 00:28:52.520
what had become of their officers,

00:28:52.520 --> 00:28:55.060
but thousands of thousands
of rank and file soldiers

00:28:55.060 --> 00:28:57.283
passed by his desk and
produced not one link

00:28:57.283 --> 00:29:00.253
to the whereabouts of a single officer.

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:03.536
Anders then ordered Czapski to find them,

00:29:03.536 --> 00:29:05.850
arming him with letters of introduction

00:29:05.850 --> 00:29:08.030
from the Commander of the Polish Army.

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:10.841
For months, he traveled
across the Soviet Union

00:29:10.841 --> 00:29:13.920
in search of news of these men.

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:15.694
But over and over again,

00:29:15.694 --> 00:29:17.640
he ran straight into a wall of silence.

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:18.683
In the meantime,

00:29:19.737 --> 00:29:21.320
Stalin was determined
to send these new Polish

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:24.068
recruits to the front, but the men,

00:29:24.068 --> 00:29:26.498
poorly fed, inadequately armed,

00:29:26.498 --> 00:29:29.529
and unable to train for combat
having just been released

00:29:29.529 --> 00:29:33.163
from a year and a half of
deprivation and squalor.

00:29:33.163 --> 00:29:35.621
Anders resisted Stalin's pressure

00:29:35.621 --> 00:29:37.979
and insisted the men be suitably recovered

00:29:37.979 --> 00:29:40.953
and trained for the
current conditions of war.

00:29:41.878 --> 00:29:46.074
By order of Anders, Czapski
was named chief officer

00:29:46.074 --> 00:29:50.340
of information and propaganda
for the Polish Army.

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:52.909
And he had the considerable distinction

00:29:52.909 --> 00:29:55.001
of being in daily contact with Anders,

00:29:55.001 --> 00:29:55.953
his commander-in-chief.

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:00.396
When it became clear that a standoff

00:30:00.396 --> 00:30:03.380
had happened in the spring of 1942,

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:05.353
Stalin agreed to let the Polish troops

00:30:05.353 --> 00:30:08.752
evacuate to Iran, where they would be fed

00:30:08.752 --> 00:30:13.752
by British rations, and
then move on to Iraq.

00:30:14.110 --> 00:30:16.710
This map shows the routes
taken by Anders' army

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:21.710
and by Czapski from his
capture in September, 1939,

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:25.093
over the course of the
next five and a half years.

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:29.323
This is an enormous undertaking
to move all of these men.

00:30:40.580 --> 00:30:43.470
Throughout the war, Czapski
continued to keep his journal,

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:45.340
making elaborate notes, drawing

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:47.900
people and scenes he witnessed.

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:52.748
On the left is an album from
these years in the middle east.

00:30:52.748 --> 00:30:56.403
Graniczhy means borderlands.

00:30:57.540 --> 00:31:00.570
Uzbekistan, Turkmenistan, Kazakhstan,

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:03.563
all of the places that the Polish army

00:31:03.563 --> 00:31:06.113
had been stationed before its exodus.

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:12.673
On the right is journal
once they arrived in Egypt.

00:31:16.490 --> 00:31:18.050
One night in Tashkent,

00:31:18.050 --> 00:31:20.300
he was invited to a literary gathering

00:31:20.300 --> 00:31:23.510
where he encountered the
Russian poet Anna Akhmatova,

00:31:23.510 --> 00:31:25.970
who's poems Czapski knew well.

00:31:25.970 --> 00:31:30.300
At this time, her poetry
was revered by many,

00:31:30.300 --> 00:31:33.950
but officially reviled,
considered decadent and bourgeois

00:31:33.950 --> 00:31:37.040
by the reigning authorities
on aesthetic taste.

00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:39.340
Her work was banned by the regime

00:31:39.340 --> 00:31:41.420
and she could no longer publish.

00:31:41.420 --> 00:31:43.900
Czapski was surprised to find her,

00:31:43.900 --> 00:31:46.500
widow of a poet murdered by the state,

00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:48.170
sitting quietly, if aloof,

00:31:48.170 --> 00:31:50.630
in a Soviet officer's private home.

00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:53.356
Czapski was asked to
read some Polish poetry,

00:31:53.356 --> 00:31:56.363
which he then translated into Russian.

00:31:57.350 --> 00:32:00.001
Ms. Akhmatova listened
intently, tears in her eyes.

00:32:00.001 --> 00:32:03.252
He gently persuaded her to
share some of her poems.

00:32:03.252 --> 00:32:06.586
Czapski learned that Stalin himself

00:32:06.586 --> 00:32:10.450
admired some of Akhmatova's work.

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:13.720
And it was he personally
who ensured her safety.

00:32:13.720 --> 00:32:16.900
In the presence of this
elegant strong woman

00:32:16.900 --> 00:32:18.800
with her sharp alert mind,

00:32:18.800 --> 00:32:21.048
Czapski found evidence
of nearly schizophrenia

00:32:21.048 --> 00:32:26.048
atmosphere imposed on the closed
society of Russian artists.

00:32:26.110 --> 00:32:29.250
Czapski was the first non-Russian

00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:31.653
Akhmatova had met in over 20 years.

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:36.410
That night they walked together,
late at night after dinner,

00:32:36.410 --> 00:32:38.563
and stayed up until dawn speaking.

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:41.920
Years later Czapski wrote that she

00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:43.720
had written a poem about that night,

00:32:44.593 --> 00:32:47.293
about the impact of his presence upon her.

00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:52.160
They met once more in 1965,

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:56.050
as she was returning from
receiving a doctorate

00:32:56.050 --> 00:32:58.940
at Oxford on her way back to Russia.

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:03.940
She had ditched her Soviet escort,

00:33:05.040 --> 00:33:07.603
had gone directly to Paris, and saw him.

00:33:16.520 --> 00:33:20.930
Czapski was in Iraq on April 13th, 1943,

00:33:20.930 --> 00:33:24.700
when radio Berlin broadcast
the news of the discovery

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:27.653
of mass graves in a
forest in Western Russia.

00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:32.940
These were some of the men, I'm sorry.

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:39.123
I'm still bringing Czapski
through the middle east here,

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:46.313
drawing as he goes, documenting his trip.

00:33:57.300 --> 00:34:00.370
Aspects of travel, aspect of daily life,

00:34:00.370 --> 00:34:05.370
and an army full of men at repose,

00:34:07.070 --> 00:34:10.343
playing soccer, bathing, showering.

00:34:17.100 --> 00:34:19.010
These are some of the men Czapski

00:34:19.010 --> 00:34:24.010
had been devoted to finding,
among them many close friends.

00:34:26.530 --> 00:34:29.853
Broadcasts from radio Berlin
had announced the graves.

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.080
This is what was dug up.

00:34:36.080 --> 00:34:41.080
These were scenes of outside
of Katyn in the forest

00:34:41.877 --> 00:34:46.877
of the bodies of Polish
officers who were still in their

00:34:47.484 --> 00:34:51.363
gray coats shot in the back of the head.

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:56.120
Forever after, for the next 50 years,

00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:58.856
Czapski would make it his duty
to remind the world of the

00:34:58.856 --> 00:35:03.663
fate of 22,000 of these
Polish officers and civilians.

00:35:08.600 --> 00:35:12.040
Polish army pushed on out of
the middle east to Jerusalem

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:15.940
and Tel Aviv, eventually
to Cairo and Port Sayeed,

00:35:15.940 --> 00:35:18.490
where they boarded ships
for the Italian peninsula.

00:35:20.117 --> 00:35:21.740
Czapski was at Anders'
side during the planning

00:35:21.740 --> 00:35:23.700
for the assault on Germans who had dug in

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:26.003
for months at monastery at Monte Cassino,

00:35:26.003 --> 00:35:30.687
ceaselessly bombed by the
Allies in hopes of dislodging

00:35:30.687 --> 00:35:34.420
them and opening the road to Rome.

00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:36.953
You can see in the picture on the bottom,

00:35:38.335 --> 00:35:41.140
the grossly remains of the monastery,

00:35:41.140 --> 00:35:44.193
which had once been such
a beautiful retreat.

00:35:50.440 --> 00:35:53.160
The Poles, in their
first foray at soldiering

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:55.020
since leaving the Soviet Union,

00:35:55.020 --> 00:35:58.340
fought valiantly and at
a great loss of life,

00:35:58.340 --> 00:36:00.930
but succeeded in uprooting the Germans.

00:36:00.930 --> 00:36:04.610
On the morning of the 18th,
the monastery was theirs.

00:36:04.610 --> 00:36:06.830
Czapski had few illusions about the Allies

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:08.620
for whom the big news that season was

00:36:08.620 --> 00:36:12.220
the invasion of Normandy
and the liberation of Paris.

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:14.380
From the Poles, still traumatized

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:17.090
by the discovery of the Katyn massacre,

00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:19.040
the most important
event of the late spring

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:23.040
of 1944 was the Warsaw uprising.

00:36:23.040 --> 00:36:24.893
Czapski's sister Maria
had remained in Warsaw

00:36:24.893 --> 00:36:27.050
through the methodical destruction

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:30.230
of most of the city by Nazi troops,

00:36:30.230 --> 00:36:33.270
As Czapski feared, all of his
work that he had stored away

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:37.187
before leaving to join his
regiment in September, 1939,

00:36:37.187 --> 00:36:39.842
his paintings, his writings, his diaries,

00:36:39.842 --> 00:36:43.113
was destroyed along with so much else.

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:47.173
His sense of unease about the
Allies proved well justified.

00:36:48.110 --> 00:36:51.280
In February of 1945 at
the Yalta conference,

00:36:51.280 --> 00:36:54.283
Stalin solidified his iron grip on Poland.

00:36:57.510 --> 00:36:59.360
Still in Rome with the Anders army,

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:03.370
Czapski published a memoir
of his prison experience.

00:37:03.370 --> 00:37:07.568
Memories of Starobielsk,
drawn from his daily journal,

00:37:07.568 --> 00:37:10.713
attended to throughout
his years as a soldier.

00:37:11.570 --> 00:37:13.680
A friend who had been a
prisoner in the labor camp

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:16.460
in Siberia the same time described

00:37:16.460 --> 00:37:18.957
Czapski's account of
life as a prisoner as,

00:37:18.957 --> 00:37:23.728
lived by a Pole, meditated
on by a European,

00:37:23.728 --> 00:37:25.633
written by a painter.

00:37:26.650 --> 00:37:29.345
The book was printed and
circulated just as the war ended

00:37:29.345 --> 00:37:32.431
and was largely ignored
or met with hostility

00:37:32.431 --> 00:37:35.263
by both Soviets and Allies.

00:37:38.710 --> 00:37:43.270
As the army of Anders
broke up in Rome in 1945,

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:48.800
Czapski and a group of
his like-minded friends

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:52.160
determined that they would create

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:56.450
an institute called the
Polish Literary Institute.

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:59.300
They decided to base it in Paris.

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:02.740
They put their funds together
and came up with a home.

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:04.610
Not this one, this is the second home,

00:38:04.610 --> 00:38:08.190
but outside of Paris in a quiet suburb

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:10.650
called Maisons-Laffitte, and this house,

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:14.940
which is almost iconic in
Russian cultural history,

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:16.810
still stands today and is still the home

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:18.593
of the Polish Literary Institute.

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:25.320
Czapski took up residence
in this upper floor,

00:38:25.320 --> 00:38:28.270
in this room with the open
window at the back of the house.

00:38:30.606 --> 00:38:32.830
It was to be his library, his salon,

00:38:32.830 --> 00:38:36.620
and his painting studio
for the next 50 years.

00:38:36.620 --> 00:38:38.860
All of the work from here on will have

00:38:38.860 --> 00:38:40.740
come out from this little room.

00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:53.244
Men like (indistinct),
shown here with Czapski,

00:38:53.244 --> 00:38:58.244
these and others after months
and months of conversations

00:39:01.031 --> 00:39:04.578
as soldiers of the Anders
army created the institute

00:39:04.578 --> 00:39:07.510
once the army was disbanded.

00:39:07.510 --> 00:39:11.860
They invested no faith in the
redemptive powers of the west.

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:15.173
They fully understood the
corrosive shadow cast at Yalta.

00:39:17.580 --> 00:39:19.320
Czapski and (indistinct),

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:23.224
and a handful of other dedicated souls,

00:39:23.224 --> 00:39:25.498
created from nothing the
magazine called Kultura.

00:39:25.498 --> 00:39:27.279
One of the most influential
and widely read journals

00:39:27.279 --> 00:39:29.580
of the 20th century.
Kultura had an astonishing

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:34.580
run of 637 issues, spanning
53 year window of time.

00:39:35.930 --> 00:39:39.760
Looking back, Kultura was
synonymous with Poland itself,

00:39:39.760 --> 00:39:43.410
acting in local (indistinct)
for the Polish institutions

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:47.033
denied the right to exist on
the ground in Poland itself.

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:51.310
The first issue shown on
the left here included works

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:55.112
by Lorca, (indistinct), Arthur Koestler.

00:39:55.112 --> 00:39:59.003
Czapski debuted with a tribute to Bonnard,

00:39:59.003 --> 00:40:00.473
who had recently died.

00:40:02.560 --> 00:40:05.136
Czapski was torn between
his cosmopolitan self

00:40:05.136 --> 00:40:09.310
and the Polish role of events
the war foisted on him.

00:40:09.310 --> 00:40:12.010
He only reluctantly embraced a nationalism

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:15.203
that ran counter to his
instinctual bohemianism.

00:40:17.420 --> 00:40:21.640
He overcame that resistance
and in his creative response

00:40:21.640 --> 00:40:25.443
to it he wrote a memoir
called Inhuman Land.

00:40:27.500 --> 00:40:30.205
You've read some of these
pages for this class.

00:40:30.205 --> 00:40:34.825
Czapski said that I was not
free to write what I wanted,

00:40:34.825 --> 00:40:38.903
but I needed to write what I must.

00:40:40.240 --> 00:40:42.490
It was difficult getting
him in that publishing

00:40:42.490 --> 00:40:45.220
reflective state of a French left's

00:40:45.220 --> 00:40:47.823
tenacious romance with Stalinism.

00:40:49.100 --> 00:40:50.800
Failing to find a publisher,

00:40:50.800 --> 00:40:53.230
Czapski's book was excerpted in the second

00:40:53.230 --> 00:40:55.033
and third issues of Kultura.

00:40:55.910 --> 00:41:00.100
Only two years later in 1949,
his old friend Daniel Halevy

00:41:00.100 --> 00:41:03.360
finally found a publisher
willing to print the entire book

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:06.013
if someone else paid for
the cost of publication.

00:41:07.850 --> 00:41:10.360
Despite encouragement from Arthur Koestler

00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:14.253
and George Orwell, no English
publisher would touch it.

00:41:19.740 --> 00:41:22.170
David Rousset was a
left-wing French journalist

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:25.410
and authority on Nazi concentration camps,

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:29.030
a survivor of Buchenwald and Neuengamme.

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:31.774
His expose of those worlds, called,

00:41:31.774 --> 00:41:34.937
The Truth About the Camps,
had been well-received,

00:41:34.937 --> 00:41:39.130
yet a review of Rousset's
book by art critic and editor

00:41:39.130 --> 00:41:43.540
Pierre Daix claimed that he was
slandering the Soviet Union,

00:41:43.540 --> 00:41:45.703
and was making false claims against it.

00:41:47.344 --> 00:41:51.280
Rousset sued for libel.

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:54.420
At the trial, Czapski
appeared as a witness,

00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:58.600
answering questions about
his years in Starobielsk.

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:01.050
The lawyers for Daix tried to undermine

00:42:01.050 --> 00:42:02.793
his credibility as a witness.

00:42:03.800 --> 00:42:06.050
Going further and holding
forth about the murders

00:42:06.050 --> 00:42:09.033
of his fellow Poles in Katyn,
Czapski stung the courtroom,

00:42:09.033 --> 00:42:14.033
enraging the defense team
and unleashing an uproar.

00:42:14.110 --> 00:42:17.003
The judge was forced to call a recess.

00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:23.480
Czapski's sphere of
influence was considerable.

00:42:23.480 --> 00:42:26.760
As he settled into Maisons-Laffitte
and a life in Paris,

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:28.540
he had access to many key players

00:42:28.540 --> 00:42:30.610
in the shaping of post-war Europe.

00:42:30.610 --> 00:42:32.920
Political, religious, cultural figures,

00:42:32.920 --> 00:42:35.490
whose doors were open to
him and who were largely

00:42:35.490 --> 00:42:39.070
sympathetic to the fates
of the Poles in exile.

00:42:39.070 --> 00:42:42.712
The sketch Czapski did
of de Gaulle, Malraux,

00:42:42.712 --> 00:42:45.500
Halevy, and Jacques-Emile Blanche,

00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:47.916
Jacques-Emile he had
met when he was in Paris

00:42:47.916 --> 00:42:49.730
in the '20s and he continued
their relationships,

00:42:49.730 --> 00:42:54.730
Camus he met and became a
colleague in publishing.

00:42:56.870 --> 00:42:59.560
Camus relied on Czapski for translations

00:42:59.560 --> 00:43:02.190
of his books in Polish, and they had

00:43:02.190 --> 00:43:06.573
a very close and personal relationship.

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:17.388
In 1946, as he began to
settle into Paris life,

00:43:17.388 --> 00:43:20.923
Czapski turned 50 just as he was about

00:43:20.923 --> 00:43:23.953
to settle into a routine painting,

00:43:25.651 --> 00:43:27.818
getting a discipline back,

00:43:29.642 --> 00:43:30.700
Dierdivitch imposed upon him to go abroad

00:43:31.908 --> 00:43:32.741
and to raise money for Kultura.

00:43:33.818 --> 00:43:36.124
So he once again put aside his brushes

00:43:36.124 --> 00:43:40.190
and traveled to South
America, to North America,

00:43:40.190 --> 00:43:43.500
to raise money for the magazine,

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:47.980
visiting both large cities
and small Polish communities.

00:43:47.980 --> 00:43:51.040
With him all the time was his sketchbook.

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:54.433
He documented all of his
trips quite thorough.

00:44:00.410 --> 00:44:04.983
Finally, in the early 1950s,

00:44:06.680 --> 00:44:09.593
Czapski begins to paint
and to paint in earnest,

00:44:10.987 --> 00:44:12.950
shifting his priorities
to the studio upstairs

00:44:13.913 --> 00:44:14.943
at the back of the house
in Maisons-Laffitte.

00:44:16.629 --> 00:44:18.129
Here is a characteristic scene

00:44:19.590 --> 00:44:20.833
that caught Czapski's
eyes and imagination.

00:44:22.520 --> 00:44:23.973
What is it that makes it
worthy of being a painting?

00:44:25.157 --> 00:44:27.683
Czapski believed that the
truth of everyday life

00:44:27.683 --> 00:44:31.250
and the truth of art were interchangeable.

00:44:31.250 --> 00:44:33.310
Unlike the generations
of Polish, religious,

00:44:33.310 --> 00:44:35.850
and historical painters who constructed

00:44:35.850 --> 00:44:36.990
heightened custom dramas,

00:44:36.990 --> 00:44:40.390
he preferred to find humbler
subjects close at hand.

00:44:40.390 --> 00:44:42.710
For Czapski, people waiting for a train

00:44:42.710 --> 00:44:45.022
were capable of revealing as
much about the metaphysics

00:44:45.022 --> 00:44:49.503
of being as any annunciation
or resurrection.

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:52.670
He did not go out in search of subjects.

00:44:52.670 --> 00:44:55.720
They appeared to him as he
went about living his life,

00:44:55.720 --> 00:44:58.200
always alert to the possible
combination of elements

00:44:58.200 --> 00:45:02.173
that might cohere into a
memorable image in his mind's eye.

00:45:03.250 --> 00:45:05.738
The scientists for whom
this Metro stop was named,

00:45:05.738 --> 00:45:09.537
Louis Pasteur, once
proclaimed about research,

00:45:09.537 --> 00:45:11.838
in the field of observation,

00:45:11.838 --> 00:45:16.838
chance favors the prepared
mind. Czapski had such a mind.

00:45:20.590 --> 00:45:23.180
Czapski declared that
the precision of the eye,

00:45:23.180 --> 00:45:25.220
not the precision of the hand,

00:45:25.220 --> 00:45:28.132
is the first and most important task.

00:45:28.132 --> 00:45:32.521
This is a variation on
the saying of Turner,

00:45:32.521 --> 00:45:35.293
paint What you see, not what you know.

00:45:36.260 --> 00:45:38.200
The result was often a canvas that,

00:45:38.200 --> 00:45:41.468
in Czapski's his own words,
could set one's teeth on edge

00:45:41.468 --> 00:45:45.560
with a subject that could as
often as not be revolting.

00:45:45.560 --> 00:45:48.030
He took his strength from Goya.

00:45:48.030 --> 00:45:51.020
He noted that a great many
criticize me for painting

00:45:51.020 --> 00:45:54.163
the filthy steps of the bazaar in Paris.

00:45:55.671 --> 00:46:00.246
He considered Woman in Front of a Train

00:46:00.246 --> 00:46:05.246
to be his masterpiece, although,

00:46:05.451 --> 00:46:07.883
that changed from time to time.

00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:11.547
Old women have a particular
fascination for him,

00:46:11.547 --> 00:46:14.290
and he admired their tactics of survival.

00:46:14.290 --> 00:46:16.090
Here, the woman is a cipher.

00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:18.223
She clearly has had our share of woe.

00:46:19.070 --> 00:46:22.191
This picture belongs to the
genre of vanitas paintings

00:46:22.191 --> 00:46:26.120
in which a painter addresses
the transiency of life,

00:46:26.120 --> 00:46:27.820
the certainty of death and the

00:46:27.820 --> 00:46:30.720
folly of pleasurable pursuits.

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:33.689
That hat, that handbag, those shoes,

00:46:33.689 --> 00:46:37.008
the fire on the painting is banked low,

00:46:37.008 --> 00:46:39.706
an orange glow at the bottom of the train,

00:46:39.706 --> 00:46:44.110
evoking a sense of embers
burning steadily inside of her.

00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:47.463
Subdued, well beyond the flames of youth.

00:46:50.670 --> 00:46:53.568
It's something of a tribute
to Czapski's iconoclasm

00:46:53.568 --> 00:46:56.183
to try to call up
another painter who draws

00:46:56.183 --> 00:47:01.183
from both Goya and Matisse
in the same painting.

00:47:01.430 --> 00:47:03.610
Here, you can see these shoes that

00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:06.626
Matisse is buckled on the left,

00:47:06.626 --> 00:47:09.230
kind of echoed on the right.

00:47:09.230 --> 00:47:12.655
So Czapski had within this
one canvas a combination

00:47:12.655 --> 00:47:17.655
of influences and yet
his own inimitable style.

00:47:22.470 --> 00:47:26.040
By the late '50s, he was
exhibiting regularly in Europe.

00:47:26.040 --> 00:47:30.793
Here he is in Paris in 1959
meeting, for the first time,

00:47:30.793 --> 00:47:33.963
the Polish poet Zbigniew Herbert.

00:47:35.220 --> 00:47:37.280
You can see the paintings scattered

00:47:37.280 --> 00:47:39.240
around the room on the wall,

00:47:39.240 --> 00:47:41.490
and you can see also
the figure of the man,

00:47:41.490 --> 00:47:45.593
how tall he is, he towers over
Herbert, and how thin he is.

00:47:51.200 --> 00:47:54.063
We'll go from now a series
of paintings from this time.

00:48:04.160 --> 00:48:09.160
These subjects range from
still-lives to interior scenes.

00:48:12.370 --> 00:48:14.900
You can imagine Czapski
coming out of the door

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:18.650
of an apartment onto the landing

00:48:18.650 --> 00:48:20.630
and looking down and seeing this.

00:48:20.630 --> 00:48:24.140
He sketched it in his diary and
made several versions of it.

00:48:24.140 --> 00:48:26.063
And this is the final painting.

00:48:28.740 --> 00:48:31.340
There's something about the headlessness

00:48:31.340 --> 00:48:36.340
of the figure that is curious and became

00:48:36.350 --> 00:48:38.053
something of a trope in his work.

00:48:44.410 --> 00:48:47.081
Again, one wonders what prompts
Czapski to capture an image

00:48:47.081 --> 00:48:50.293
many of us might otherwise
have turned away from.

00:48:51.500 --> 00:48:56.500
He claims to have come across
an absolute absence of bias,

00:48:56.530 --> 00:48:58.830
a willingness to know and understand

00:48:58.830 --> 00:49:02.260
as many opposing states of
the human soul as possible,

00:49:02.260 --> 00:49:05.127
a capacity for discovering
in the lowest of man

00:49:05.127 --> 00:49:08.980
such nobility as to appear sublime,

00:49:08.980 --> 00:49:13.793
and in the seemingly purest of
beings, the basest instincts.

00:49:15.250 --> 00:49:18.623
Czapski shares this breadth
of curiosity and fascination.

00:49:22.900 --> 00:49:26.053
A sequence of entries on
the pages of his diary.

00:49:27.010 --> 00:49:30.660
I bought a whole bunch of
lilies, sometimes my own vision,

00:49:30.660 --> 00:49:33.410
which being ravished has intensified,

00:49:33.410 --> 00:49:35.063
almost forcing itself on me,

00:49:36.232 --> 00:49:38.460
frees me, unifies me internally.

00:49:38.460 --> 00:49:41.740
But for that to happen I
need access to deeper waters

00:49:41.740 --> 00:49:44.420
to counter the flow of the ravishment.

00:49:44.420 --> 00:49:46.833
These waters, I haven't gotten.

00:49:47.840 --> 00:49:50.527
How often does this ravishment
improve a sterile ground,

00:49:50.527 --> 00:49:54.200
or cause a failure in a back and down?

00:49:54.200 --> 00:49:56.440
I wait, not having the power in me

00:49:57.353 --> 00:49:59.640
to make this leap forward,
to attack the canvas.

00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:03.270
I want to paint the lilies
in a very modest picture,

00:50:03.270 --> 00:50:07.097
to apply in a single go
some perfect juxtapositions

00:50:07.097 --> 00:50:12.050
of brown, of velvety red,
the green of the leaves,

00:50:12.050 --> 00:50:15.870
the white of the flower,
all of that is so beautiful.

00:50:15.870 --> 00:50:16.853
It stops me.

00:50:18.500 --> 00:50:22.517
Czapski was always a painter of doubt.

00:50:22.517 --> 00:50:23.720
He doubted his own abilities,

00:50:23.720 --> 00:50:26.740
and he doubted what he was able to produce

00:50:26.740 --> 00:50:29.143
and whether or not it had value.

00:50:30.349 --> 00:50:32.414
He was very hard on himself.

00:50:32.414 --> 00:50:37.340
And yet this struggle, this
internal struggle, urged him on.

00:50:37.340 --> 00:50:39.540
He had an enormous furnace with him,

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:43.800
burning with the desire
to remove the vision

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:46.313
from within him and put it onto the page.

00:50:50.100 --> 00:50:51.950
Painting of his sister, (indistinct).

00:50:55.036 --> 00:50:58.260
He did portraits. He
had people sit for him

00:50:59.283 --> 00:51:02.250
to keep his discipline, his hand.

00:51:02.250 --> 00:51:06.120
He disliked formal commission portraiture,

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:09.984
but he did it knowing that it
could bring positive results

00:51:09.984 --> 00:51:13.223
in terms of commissioned work or sales.

00:51:16.780 --> 00:51:18.600
Here are two formal portraits he did.

00:51:18.600 --> 00:51:21.290
One of Herbert and the other of

00:51:21.290 --> 00:51:23.663
the Portuguese writer Ferrata Mora.

00:51:24.550 --> 00:51:26.850
The fascination with the hands

00:51:26.850 --> 00:51:30.433
of the writers is, to
me, of great interest.

00:51:41.360 --> 00:51:43.990
This is a self portrait in which Czapski

00:51:43.990 --> 00:51:45.980
looks out at us circumspectly,

00:51:45.980 --> 00:51:49.373
not quite open faced,
but present, handsome.

00:51:50.664 --> 00:51:53.022
Without his truncated
eyeglasses, he's unflustered,

00:51:53.022 --> 00:51:56.310
suggesting, my glasses have broken?

00:51:56.310 --> 00:51:58.193
Well, yes. Okay, it's not a big deal.

00:51:59.170 --> 00:52:01.390
The picture he's looking out of is shown

00:52:01.390 --> 00:52:03.390
at the easel on the right.

00:52:03.390 --> 00:52:06.120
Everything appears rather matter of fact

00:52:06.120 --> 00:52:08.321
with the light bulb floating, swimming

00:52:08.321 --> 00:52:10.450
like a Monet Waterlily in a lake

00:52:10.450 --> 00:52:12.633
of luminous effects behind his head.

00:52:13.470 --> 00:52:15.540
Is the subject of the
painting the painter's head

00:52:15.540 --> 00:52:18.930
or the bulb, or the space between them?

00:52:18.930 --> 00:52:21.264
There seems to be an
acknowledgement of shared territory,

00:52:21.264 --> 00:52:26.264
a fullness and of emptiness,
of being and nothingness,

00:52:28.930 --> 00:52:31.440
existing together at one side-by-side,

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:34.450
neither one state nor
the other prevailing.

00:52:34.450 --> 00:52:37.900
The atmosphere of the painting,
fueled by almost ecstatic

00:52:37.900 --> 00:52:41.063
verse strokes put down
with great effervescence,

00:52:41.063 --> 00:52:43.780
raises existential questions after

00:52:43.780 --> 00:52:46.490
the fashion of Beckett and Giacometti,

00:52:46.490 --> 00:52:48.750
both of whom exerted powerful influences

00:52:48.750 --> 00:52:50.323
on Czapski at this time.

00:52:56.810 --> 00:52:59.830
He was able to catch psychological states,

00:52:59.830 --> 00:53:04.240
often pulling out of the sitter something

00:53:04.240 --> 00:53:06.950
that perhaps they didn't mean to reveal.

00:53:06.950 --> 00:53:10.225
The painting on the right
is one of the first examples

00:53:10.225 --> 00:53:13.400
that would become the genre for Czapski

00:53:13.400 --> 00:53:17.160
of painting people looking at paintings.

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:20.470
Somehow he became so
involved with the work

00:53:20.470 --> 00:53:24.090
that he was at museums,
he would go to galleries,

00:53:24.090 --> 00:53:25.390
and not only the work itself,

00:53:25.390 --> 00:53:28.020
but the engagement of
the people with the work

00:53:28.020 --> 00:53:30.050
of art became significant to him

00:53:30.050 --> 00:53:32.920
and became a subject of many works.

00:53:32.920 --> 00:53:34.980
Here, a young man stands in front

00:53:34.980 --> 00:53:38.593
of an abstract painting
by Nicholas de Stael.

00:53:42.490 --> 00:53:47.090
It is an example of his
attempt to reconcile

00:53:47.090 --> 00:53:50.810
non-representational
abstraction with his own work,

00:53:50.810 --> 00:53:53.940
putting up an abstract
painting into a painting

00:53:53.940 --> 00:53:56.510
of the figure so that you get both.

00:53:56.510 --> 00:54:00.470
This is a time in Paris where abstraction,

00:54:00.470 --> 00:54:02.500
non-representational expression,

00:54:02.500 --> 00:54:06.471
became almost (indistinct),
and Czapski resisted it

00:54:06.471 --> 00:54:10.720
for many reasons, for as long as he could.

00:54:10.720 --> 00:54:13.420
He tried to understand it,
but he was not drawn to it.

00:54:16.380 --> 00:54:18.890
Here, his sketches of
people looking at works

00:54:18.890 --> 00:54:22.363
that he did in museums and
galleries throughout Paris.

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:31.370
His books were filled with such drawings.

00:54:33.905 --> 00:54:37.260
Here in the center is
a self portrait of him

00:54:37.260 --> 00:54:39.193
as an older man looking at a painting.

00:54:44.120 --> 00:54:47.370
The awareness of himself and others

00:54:47.370 --> 00:54:50.773
as they engaged with works of art.

00:54:56.400 --> 00:55:01.400
This painting was done
after visiting a Corbett

00:55:01.550 --> 00:55:03.663
retrospective at the Grand Palais.

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:09.611
Czapski gets the opportunity to paint

00:55:09.611 --> 00:55:13.258
not only what he sees, but to
paint the Corbett's as well.

00:55:13.258 --> 00:55:16.158
And to reduce them to a certain
kind of essence of vision.

00:55:29.140 --> 00:55:32.980
The technique of brushing
so loosely in this

00:55:32.980 --> 00:55:37.980
was at a time when the same free immersion

00:55:41.656 --> 00:55:45.770
in what might be called
abstract expressionists'

00:55:45.770 --> 00:55:49.683
technique was happening in New York.

00:55:50.780 --> 00:55:53.460
Painters like Philip
Guston and Grace Hartigan

00:55:53.460 --> 00:55:58.460
embodied the use of that
technique with narrative

00:55:58.571 --> 00:56:02.813
paintings that is very
close to Czapski's heart.

00:56:03.740 --> 00:56:08.740
He did not know their
work. Here's an example.

00:56:08.780 --> 00:56:11.890
A detail of the way
the paint was put down.

00:56:11.890 --> 00:56:15.393
Every brushstroke, you must
remember, requires a decision.

00:56:22.990 --> 00:56:26.634
Vision, Czapski wrote, is a
shock where willful choice

00:56:26.634 --> 00:56:30.090
between this or that is powerless.

00:56:30.090 --> 00:56:33.240
A miracle of beauty,
which one alone can see,

00:56:33.240 --> 00:56:37.020
unique, fleeting, imperceptible,

00:56:37.020 --> 00:56:39.253
or even held up to ridicule by others.

00:56:41.870 --> 00:56:46.313
Very characteristic
choice of subject matter.

00:56:47.320 --> 00:56:49.433
The sense of presence and absence,

00:56:53.550 --> 00:56:56.430
the hope of transcendence and the spark

00:56:56.430 --> 00:56:58.833
of those yellow orange dabs of paint.

00:57:10.997 --> 00:57:13.840
In 1980, Czapski turned 84.

00:57:13.840 --> 00:57:16.290
He continued to work attacking the canvas

00:57:16.290 --> 00:57:18.733
with more gusto and buoyant energy.

00:57:19.653 --> 00:57:22.150
Painting was a necessity for him,

00:57:22.150 --> 00:57:25.140
as essential as breathing and thinking.

00:57:25.140 --> 00:57:27.540
His eyesight began to diminish radically,

00:57:27.540 --> 00:57:30.410
but his appetite for
work was unquenchable.

00:57:30.410 --> 00:57:32.180
As is true from (indistinct),

00:57:32.180 --> 00:57:33.920
encroaching blindness spurred him

00:57:34.849 --> 00:57:36.480
on to new depths of seeing.

00:57:36.480 --> 00:57:38.540
He kept his tubes of paint in envelopes

00:57:38.540 --> 00:57:41.376
with the name of the color
scrawled in his large hand

00:57:41.376 --> 00:57:44.577
to facilitate identification as he worked.

00:57:45.510 --> 00:57:47.060
In his book, Late Style,

00:57:47.060 --> 00:57:49.190
the critic Edward Said wrote,

00:57:49.190 --> 00:57:51.180
late style is what happens if art

00:57:51.180 --> 00:57:54.602
does not abdicate its
rights in favor of reality.

00:57:54.602 --> 00:57:57.330
The paintings Czapski
made in his final years

00:57:57.330 --> 00:57:59.630
reveal a harnessing of his vision

00:57:59.630 --> 00:58:02.490
and a clarity of purpose few can match.

00:58:02.490 --> 00:58:04.610
In a time of physical decline,

00:58:04.610 --> 00:58:07.030
he was somehow gaining momentum.

00:58:07.030 --> 00:58:09.250
He persisted in his quixotic fashion.

00:58:09.250 --> 00:58:12.310
Each canvas was a testimony and a victory.

00:58:12.310 --> 00:58:15.340
When his beloved sister Maria died,

00:58:15.340 --> 00:58:17.930
he wrote in his diary, before I die,

00:58:17.930 --> 00:58:19.728
I would like to have a time of painting

00:58:19.728 --> 00:58:22.322
in the high heat of work.

00:58:22.322 --> 00:58:26.513
He did have his time and he
worked it for all it was worth.

00:58:28.450 --> 00:58:30.490
He began a series of landscape paintings

00:58:31.423 --> 00:58:34.093
in the early '80s as his
eyesight was beginning to wane.

00:58:41.540 --> 00:58:43.790
In this canvas called Cloudburst,

00:58:43.790 --> 00:58:47.040
the trembling sky holds a
gathering of meteorological

00:58:47.040 --> 00:58:50.713
factors, a highly dramatic,
almost operatic scene.

00:58:52.630 --> 00:58:54.900
Here, atmosphere has
been triggered to change.

00:58:54.900 --> 00:58:59.580
The sky is vaporous,
animated, insubstantial.

00:58:59.580 --> 00:59:01.020
A puff ball of cloud hovers,

00:59:01.020 --> 00:59:04.853
sparking a kind of enchantment
like a Shakespearean sprite.

00:59:06.200 --> 00:59:08.320
Something magical emerges.

00:59:08.320 --> 00:59:10.000
Czapski wrote to a friend,

00:59:10.000 --> 00:59:12.580
what's surprising is
that I'm still painting.

00:59:12.580 --> 00:59:13.950
Each time I start a canvas,

00:59:13.950 --> 00:59:16.410
I have the feeling that
I'm fooling the audience

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:18.213
since I can see almost nothing.

00:59:30.810 --> 00:59:33.930
The touch of Czapski's brush is fearless

00:59:33.930 --> 00:59:35.975
whipping across the canvas

00:59:35.975 --> 00:59:37.430
like the wind blowing over a field.

00:59:37.430 --> 00:59:39.110
Diminished eyesight freed him

00:59:39.110 --> 00:59:41.550
from a certain tyranny of form.

00:59:41.550 --> 00:59:44.190
It's as though he is letting
his brush do the seeing.

00:59:44.190 --> 00:59:47.910
Loading it up with color he
let's it play across the canvas,

00:59:47.910 --> 00:59:49.500
seeming to show him what it is

00:59:49.500 --> 00:59:52.000
he's seeing as it moves along.

00:59:52.000 --> 00:59:55.438
Several of the landscapes
do verge on pastoral cliche,

00:59:55.438 --> 00:59:57.350
but his response to what he sees

00:59:57.350 --> 00:59:59.790
is always free and unencumbered.

00:59:59.790 --> 01:00:03.773
The painting seem to inhale
vision and exhale color.

01:00:05.620 --> 01:00:07.670
Czapski looked out on a moral universe.

01:00:07.670 --> 01:00:10.070
He was never indifferent to what he saw.

01:00:10.070 --> 01:00:12.932
Trees and fields spoke their truth to him,

01:00:12.932 --> 01:00:15.930
revealing in a way you
could be in the world

01:00:15.930 --> 01:00:18.223
beyond the storied failings of man.

01:00:19.460 --> 01:00:22.000
He spent his summers in
(indistinct) walking every day,

01:00:22.000 --> 01:00:23.950
sketchbook in hand, striding out among

01:00:23.950 --> 01:00:27.000
the open farmland and rolling hills.

01:00:27.000 --> 01:00:29.110
Landscapes he painted in these years

01:00:29.110 --> 01:00:31.860
presented allegorical
marriage of the bucolic

01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:34.500
and the impenetrable, embodying an almost

01:00:34.500 --> 01:00:37.763
biblical tension between
innocence and knowledge.

01:00:39.330 --> 01:00:42.793
The process was referred to
by the critic William Epson

01:00:42.793 --> 01:00:46.433
as putting the complex into the simple.

01:00:47.910 --> 01:00:49.490
These works are unselfconscious

01:00:49.490 --> 01:00:52.763
in a new way, poetic, empathic, pure.

01:01:01.323 --> 01:01:06.323
Here's Czapski in his room,
with a self portrait done.

01:01:08.510 --> 01:01:12.670
You'll see on the far right
side of the photograph,

01:01:12.670 --> 01:01:15.830
the lower shelf has stacks of books

01:01:15.830 --> 01:01:17.410
leaning up one against each other.

01:01:17.410 --> 01:01:21.200
These are his diaries that
he always kept at hand.

01:01:21.200 --> 01:01:23.810
He slept in the bed he's sitting on now

01:01:23.810 --> 01:01:26.190
with his head to the
wall and every morning

01:01:26.190 --> 01:01:28.190
he would reach up and grab at random

01:01:28.190 --> 01:01:31.490
one of those journal volumes.

01:01:31.490 --> 01:01:35.220
He would read what he had written
at some point in the past.

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:37.760
And he would often annotate his response

01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:41.850
to what he had read and
then find the latest journal

01:01:41.850 --> 01:01:44.963
and begin his day fresh with new musings.

01:01:49.520 --> 01:01:53.370
In 1981, martial law
was declared in Poland.

01:01:53.370 --> 01:01:56.830
Solidarity was banned and many
of its leaders were jailed.

01:01:56.830 --> 01:01:58.870
A black and white
photograph of five prisoners

01:01:58.870 --> 01:02:01.230
walking on a routine exercise break

01:02:01.230 --> 01:02:03.930
under a makeshift knitting
of prison security

01:02:03.930 --> 01:02:06.390
was smuggled out of Poland
and made the rounds,

01:02:06.390 --> 01:02:08.800
distributed to the world's press.

01:02:08.800 --> 01:02:11.100
Czapski was moved to make
a painting of something

01:02:11.100 --> 01:02:14.010
he had not seen with his own eyes.

01:02:14.010 --> 01:02:16.448
He had left Krakow for Paris in 1924

01:02:16.448 --> 01:02:19.680
to sever all relations
with the overblown style

01:02:19.680 --> 01:02:23.360
of historical painting, but
here he made one of his own,

01:02:23.360 --> 01:02:26.760
limiting his palette to the
mixture of black and white.

01:02:26.760 --> 01:02:28.920
Draining all color out
of the image also hinted

01:02:28.920 --> 01:02:30.870
at the Western European idea of life

01:02:30.870 --> 01:02:34.960
in a Soviet satellite
nation, a dismal prospect.

01:02:34.960 --> 01:02:39.847
Bleak, dystopian, the feeders
appear weary and vulnerable.

01:02:39.847 --> 01:02:42.890
For 40 years, this painting
hung in the hallway

01:02:42.890 --> 01:02:45.463
leading to Czapski's
room in Maisons-Laffitte.

01:02:48.350 --> 01:02:50.100
I like to think of this late canvas

01:02:51.022 --> 01:02:53.240
as a companion piece to
Titian's great late painting,

01:02:53.240 --> 01:02:55.490
the Flaying of Marsyas.

01:02:55.490 --> 01:02:59.870
Full of emotional strength,
exuding negative capability.

01:02:59.870 --> 01:03:02.200
The narrative elements are all there,

01:03:02.200 --> 01:03:05.990
but as Morandi's quiet still lives,

01:03:05.990 --> 01:03:08.213
the picture is hardly about any of them.

01:03:10.007 --> 01:03:11.613
The surface is all rough and stumbled.

01:03:12.489 --> 01:03:14.260
Almost whimsical, the
picture also gives us

01:03:14.260 --> 01:03:15.970
the strong whiff of what the French

01:03:15.970 --> 01:03:17.097
call (speaking in French),

01:03:18.220 --> 01:03:22.160
the kind of progressive
awareness of one's mortality.

01:03:22.160 --> 01:03:25.773
We see the painters thighs and
knees, the tips of his shoes.

01:03:26.910 --> 01:03:29.170
He's engaged in viewing
an exhibit of paintings.

01:03:29.170 --> 01:03:31.930
Each reduced to a crude
rectangle with a yellow line

01:03:31.930 --> 01:03:34.610
around it indicating a frame.

01:03:34.610 --> 01:03:36.600
The particular paintings hung on display

01:03:36.600 --> 01:03:39.553
don't matter any more than
the identity of the painter.

01:03:41.100 --> 01:03:43.940
The painting is a study
of Czapski's sacred space,

01:03:43.940 --> 01:03:46.590
his temple, his place of worship,

01:03:46.590 --> 01:03:48.480
his attempt to delineate the boundless

01:03:48.480 --> 01:03:50.650
boundaries of the realm of art,

01:03:50.650 --> 01:03:54.350
a vast and anonymous
expanse of the imagination,

01:03:54.350 --> 01:03:57.323
greater than any specific
work or individual.

01:03:58.640 --> 01:04:00.440
In the '50s, Czapski had painted

01:04:00.440 --> 01:04:02.677
a subceded soprano singing (indistinct).

01:04:07.460 --> 01:04:11.820
Here, 35 years later, he
painted himself, as it were,

01:04:11.820 --> 01:04:16.483
singing the same, and is
both audience and performer.

01:04:17.530 --> 01:04:19.733
The gallery has no fourth wall.

01:04:24.200 --> 01:04:27.510
One of his latest paintings done.

01:04:27.510 --> 01:04:30.673
You can see this. His eyesight
is really very minimal.

01:04:32.310 --> 01:04:33.593
There's a detail of it.

01:04:35.027 --> 01:04:36.750
In an essay called What
Abstract Art Means To Me,

01:04:36.750 --> 01:04:39.431
Willem de Kooning conjectured that art

01:04:39.431 --> 01:04:41.930
used to be about what
would put in a picture.

01:04:41.930 --> 01:04:44.000
Then the time came when
the question was not

01:04:44.000 --> 01:04:46.983
of what one could paint,
but what one could not.

01:04:48.320 --> 01:04:51.764
He went on to say that subject matter

01:04:51.764 --> 01:04:55.260
is the forever mute part of the picture.

01:04:55.260 --> 01:04:56.803
You can talk about it forever.

01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:05.260
Here's Czapski in his room in his studio

01:05:05.260 --> 01:05:08.710
with his desk, with his
diary, with his paintings

01:05:08.710 --> 01:05:10.290
crammed one up against another,

01:05:10.290 --> 01:05:14.023
fearless, going on full bore.

01:05:21.886 --> 01:05:24.390
Czapski never understood why
he had not suffered the same

01:05:24.390 --> 01:05:26.950
tragic end of his fellow prisoners.

01:05:26.950 --> 01:05:28.540
Two years before his death,

01:05:28.540 --> 01:05:32.743
a year after the fall of communism,

01:05:32.743 --> 01:05:36.910
he was informed by a
Russian historian in 1991

01:05:36.910 --> 01:05:39.512
that a certain Countess Patapsco

01:05:39.512 --> 01:05:41.162
and Count Ferdinand (indistinct),

01:05:42.510 --> 01:05:44.740
who had held sway with
the German high command

01:05:44.740 --> 01:05:48.400
during the war, had
interceded to save his life.

01:05:48.400 --> 01:05:51.170
They convinced the officers
that people born in the areas

01:05:51.170 --> 01:05:53.050
annexed by Nazi Germany fell under

01:05:53.050 --> 01:05:55.460
the jurisdiction of the Third Reich.

01:05:55.460 --> 01:05:57.500
Czapski was born in Prague.

01:05:57.500 --> 01:06:00.450
As part of that, apparently
their influence was strong

01:06:00.450 --> 01:06:03.730
enough to have the command
agree and they may have

01:06:03.730 --> 01:06:06.193
added his name to a list
of those to be spared.

01:06:07.210 --> 01:06:09.620
The MKVD received the list from German

01:06:09.620 --> 01:06:12.530
embassy on March 5th, 1940,

01:06:12.530 --> 01:06:14.850
the very day the
Politburo signed the order

01:06:14.850 --> 01:06:18.740
leading to the murder of
22,000 Polish officers

01:06:20.250 --> 01:06:23.833
just a month before the executions began.

01:06:25.550 --> 01:06:28.170
Czapski didn't recognize
either of the two names.

01:06:28.170 --> 01:06:30.730
Though it may well be true,

01:06:30.730 --> 01:06:33.530
this report is ultimately unverifiable.

01:06:33.530 --> 01:06:35.670
So many people wanted to know why Czapski

01:06:35.670 --> 01:06:37.953
was not among those who
would be sacrificed,

01:06:38.800 --> 01:06:40.670
but in my opinion, appearing on a list

01:06:40.670 --> 01:06:44.420
of lives to be spared, the Polish officer,

01:06:44.420 --> 01:06:47.610
and an titled aristocrat to boot,

01:06:47.610 --> 01:06:49.270
would have seemed more likely to have made

01:06:49.270 --> 01:06:52.293
the MKVD want to kill him than otherwise.

01:06:56.840 --> 01:06:58.993
These are Czapski's late journals,

01:07:01.070 --> 01:07:03.770
bought at an art supply store in Paris,

01:07:03.770 --> 01:07:05.900
where coincidentally I was also

01:07:05.900 --> 01:07:08.150
living and bought my supplies.

01:07:08.150 --> 01:07:12.390
Same time in the '80s and late '70s.

01:07:15.990 --> 01:07:17.440
A few days before his death,

01:07:17.440 --> 01:07:21.290
he scribbled in an awkward
handwriting of a blind man,

01:07:21.290 --> 01:07:26.063
the words Bonnard,
Matisse, Goya, and Proust.

01:07:27.140 --> 01:07:29.003
And then in capital letters,

01:07:30.053 --> 01:07:31.943
across many pages, the word Katyn.

01:07:36.400 --> 01:07:40.000
Here on the last page of his
nearly 300 surviving volumes

01:07:40.000 --> 01:07:43.350
of his lifelong journal
are his final marks,

01:07:43.350 --> 01:07:45.490
the strokings of a pen on a sheet of paper

01:07:45.490 --> 01:07:48.164
by a blind man in search of reassurance

01:07:48.164 --> 01:07:51.540
that making a mark is a valuable activity,

01:07:51.540 --> 01:07:54.443
that it makes a difference. Thank you.

01:07:58.720 --> 01:08:02.779
- Thank you, Eric, for
bringing to our audience

01:08:02.779 --> 01:08:07.779
and to our class a sense
of the man and of his work

01:08:10.180 --> 01:08:14.476
that otherwise would
certainly have escaped us.

01:08:14.476 --> 01:08:19.476
Thank you for bringing to bear
so humbly and so persuasively

01:08:20.162 --> 01:08:25.162
your skills as a historian
of art and as an artist.

01:08:26.190 --> 01:08:29.500
I feel like from this, we have
what I'd hoped we would have,

01:08:29.500 --> 01:08:32.630
which is an entirely
different angle of view,

01:08:32.630 --> 01:08:37.018
which is itself about
what it means to see,

01:08:37.018 --> 01:08:42.018
which was Czapski's
lifelong preoccupation.

01:08:43.490 --> 01:08:46.032
I think I forgot when I introduced you

01:08:46.032 --> 01:08:48.700
to remind the audience that the way

01:08:48.700 --> 01:08:52.963
we ask questions is by putting your name,

01:08:55.002 --> 01:08:56.640
someone correct me if necessary,

01:08:56.640 --> 01:09:00.950
using the question and
answer function down below,

01:09:00.950 --> 01:09:05.950
or by using the chat and
writing down questions.

01:09:06.930 --> 01:09:09.060
I didn't tell anyone this so I don't

01:09:09.060 --> 01:09:11.040
think I have any questions yet,

01:09:11.040 --> 01:09:14.920
which opens up for me the possibility

01:09:14.920 --> 01:09:17.100
of abusing the privilege of the chair

01:09:17.100 --> 01:09:18.613
and asking you the first one.

01:09:20.370 --> 01:09:22.220
I'm going to give the students time

01:09:22.220 --> 01:09:24.639
to think about how they might ask

01:09:24.639 --> 01:09:26.733
questions related to the class.

01:09:27.650 --> 01:09:31.050
One thing which, you know, is fairly clear

01:09:31.050 --> 01:09:34.094
in your presentation, although
you don't say it outright,

01:09:34.094 --> 01:09:37.083
is that Czapski got better.

01:09:39.381 --> 01:09:43.150
He was better in middle-age as a painter

01:09:43.150 --> 01:09:48.110
than he was as a young
man, I believe fair to say.

01:09:48.110 --> 01:09:51.010
And then one could even
say that as a very old man,

01:09:51.010 --> 01:09:52.919
he might've been better than

01:09:52.919 --> 01:09:55.580
he was when he was in middle age.

01:09:55.580 --> 01:09:58.370
And so I want to ask you if I'm
understanding you correctly.

01:09:58.370 --> 01:10:00.210
I mean, I realize that
better than, worse than,

01:10:00.210 --> 01:10:05.210
are not words that are just
reached to with alacrity,

01:10:05.293 --> 01:10:07.460
but forgive my primitive
way of putting it.

01:10:07.460 --> 01:10:10.578
I want to ask you if I'm
understanding you correctly.

01:10:10.578 --> 01:10:13.853
And I also want to ask
you your views about why.

01:10:14.850 --> 01:10:17.266
Of course, in the context of this class,

01:10:17.266 --> 01:10:22.266
we're thinking about Soviet
camps and incarceration

01:10:23.100 --> 01:10:25.980
in general and experience and Czapski

01:10:25.980 --> 01:10:27.980
does have this, you know,
there's a striking fact

01:10:27.980 --> 01:10:32.980
that he can't really
paint for roughly a decade

01:10:34.210 --> 01:10:35.850
because of the Second World War

01:10:35.850 --> 01:10:37.940
and the things he felt called upon to do,

01:10:37.940 --> 01:10:40.880
and that he returns to
painting after 10 years.

01:10:40.880 --> 01:10:42.580
And that he's much more interesting,

01:10:42.580 --> 01:10:44.990
I think, when he returns to it.

01:10:44.990 --> 01:10:48.290
That must have something to do
with not being able to paint.

01:10:48.290 --> 01:10:50.530
It must have something
to do with the camps.

01:10:50.530 --> 01:10:51.810
And I guess the riddle would be,

01:10:51.810 --> 01:10:53.900
or the question I want
to put you directly is,

01:10:53.900 --> 01:10:56.270
what is your feeling
about that relationship?

01:10:56.270 --> 01:11:00.173
Like, in some sense, Czapski as a creator,

01:11:02.490 --> 01:11:03.983
with the few exceptions,
one of which you showed,

01:11:05.052 --> 01:11:06.950
the 1981 martial law painting.

01:11:06.950 --> 01:11:09.160
He's not someone who is, you know,

01:11:09.160 --> 01:11:14.160
except when he is directly
rendering something in the camps,

01:11:15.230 --> 01:11:16.720
he's not a painter who's bringing

01:11:16.720 --> 01:11:19.040
the camps out into the world, right?

01:11:19.040 --> 01:11:21.040
So there's not that kind of simple

01:11:21.040 --> 01:11:24.010
relationship, but there must be some.

01:11:24.010 --> 01:11:26.630
And I wonder if in all of
your thinking about this,

01:11:26.630 --> 01:11:28.000
you would share with us what you think

01:11:28.000 --> 01:11:30.483
that relationship might've been?

01:11:32.020 --> 01:11:34.182
- There are a few questions in there,

01:11:34.182 --> 01:11:38.753
and I will try if I can to answer

01:11:38.753 --> 01:11:41.410
them in the order they were asked.

01:11:41.410 --> 01:11:46.410
About whether or not Czapski
got better as a painter.

01:11:48.160 --> 01:11:53.160
I feel that Czapski was
enormously privileged

01:11:53.280 --> 01:11:57.500
to be able to live 97 years, you know.

01:11:57.500 --> 01:12:00.704
He had the arc of a painter that

01:12:00.704 --> 01:12:05.264
is quite considerable,
and he was a slow starter.

01:12:05.264 --> 01:12:09.070
When he was in Paris,
he felt very frustrated

01:12:09.070 --> 01:12:11.130
because the group of Polish painters

01:12:11.130 --> 01:12:13.950
around him all made enormous strides,

01:12:13.950 --> 01:12:17.645
and he felt that he
was constantly treading

01:12:17.645 --> 01:12:22.270
in place and not making progress.

01:12:22.270 --> 01:12:23.980
By the time he got back to Warsaw,

01:12:23.980 --> 01:12:27.670
I think he had an increase
in confidence in himself.

01:12:27.670 --> 01:12:30.740
I think some of this is
about one's relationship

01:12:30.740 --> 01:12:34.630
to one's taking on a lifetime of painting

01:12:34.630 --> 01:12:38.150
and whether or not one is
qualified to actually do that.

01:12:38.150 --> 01:12:42.192
And I think by the time
he got back to Warsaw

01:12:42.192 --> 01:12:46.536
in the '30s, he felt emboldened.

01:12:46.536 --> 01:12:51.310
The companion experience
of writing about painting,

01:12:51.310 --> 01:12:54.670
I think, freed up a
certain part of division

01:12:54.670 --> 01:12:57.170
in him because he could talk about it

01:12:57.170 --> 01:12:59.740
and almost extract it from his mind

01:12:59.740 --> 01:13:01.943
that freed his eyes to see something.

01:13:03.100 --> 01:13:07.290
So he was developing and he was
finding what his subject is,

01:13:07.290 --> 01:13:10.829
the same way a singer
finds his voice, I think.

01:13:10.829 --> 01:13:13.773
And it was a very slow process,

01:13:13.773 --> 01:13:17.610
which was brutally interrupted
just as it was beginning

01:13:17.610 --> 01:13:22.610
to kind of coalesce when he left in 1939.

01:13:24.690 --> 01:13:28.220
It was a very sterile
decade that followed.

01:13:28.220 --> 01:13:31.299
In terms of painting, he painted one

01:13:31.299 --> 01:13:33.925
painting in Baghdad that remains

01:13:33.925 --> 01:13:38.200
a landscape of a street scene in Baghdad.

01:13:38.200 --> 01:13:43.200
But primarily he was reduced
to pen and pencil and paper,

01:13:43.430 --> 01:13:46.110
and he made enormous use of that.

01:13:46.110 --> 01:13:51.110
I think the lack of paint
enabled him to embody

01:13:54.669 --> 01:13:57.952
what for him had always
been the ideal that Saison

01:13:57.952 --> 01:14:02.952
determined was that you
should draw with color,

01:14:04.230 --> 01:14:07.620
draw directly with color rather than draw

01:14:07.620 --> 01:14:10.370
with line and then paint in with color.

01:14:10.370 --> 01:14:13.600
And this is all of those
10 years of growing

01:14:13.600 --> 01:14:18.070
enabled him to see the
confidence that he could have,

01:14:18.070 --> 01:14:20.790
that he could ultimately,
when he got back to paint,

01:14:20.790 --> 01:14:25.260
he could just jump in and
paint directly with the color.

01:14:25.260 --> 01:14:27.170
And that's in the '50s when his work

01:14:27.170 --> 01:14:32.150
really begins to go off, I
think he was at that point

01:14:32.150 --> 01:14:36.410
not so much concerned about the quality

01:14:36.410 --> 01:14:40.410
of the work as it was
the subject of the work.

01:14:40.410 --> 01:14:42.680
What was he being called to paint?

01:14:42.680 --> 01:14:44.440
And this brings up another part

01:14:44.440 --> 01:14:46.473
of the question that you had,

01:14:52.770 --> 01:14:57.770
how does a painter determine
what it is that his subject is?

01:14:59.170 --> 01:15:03.673
And it took a very long time
for Czapski to find this,

01:15:04.530 --> 01:15:06.570
but it was ongoing and all the time.

01:15:06.570 --> 01:15:09.590
And it was about the doubt that he had

01:15:09.590 --> 01:15:12.729
internally that he would try
one subject after another.

01:15:12.729 --> 01:15:14.760
But I think that you're correct

01:15:14.760 --> 01:15:16.574
in saying that as he got older,

01:15:16.574 --> 01:15:20.690
the forces began to come together in him.

01:15:20.690 --> 01:15:25.610
And he somehow, I think with the beginning

01:15:25.610 --> 01:15:28.554
of the loss of sight, when he turned 80,

01:15:28.554 --> 01:15:33.554
the 15 years at the end of his life

01:15:33.820 --> 01:15:37.210
were the most remarkable
late flowering of painting

01:15:37.210 --> 01:15:41.300
that I can imagine because not only

01:15:41.300 --> 01:15:42.810
was it a maturing of a vision,

01:15:42.810 --> 01:15:46.527
but it was really the beginning
of confidence as a painter,

01:15:46.527 --> 01:15:50.099
and the body of work from 1980 to 1990

01:15:50.099 --> 01:15:55.099
is both massive and empowered.

01:15:55.660 --> 01:16:00.660
And as I said about Edward Said saying

01:16:01.690 --> 01:16:05.639
that it is a late style that has to do

01:16:05.639 --> 01:16:10.120
with commitment to art
rather than reality.

01:16:10.120 --> 01:16:12.450
And so he was able to give up the things

01:16:12.450 --> 01:16:15.160
that had held him back
before about naturalism

01:16:15.160 --> 01:16:19.440
or about vision and just to embody

01:16:19.440 --> 01:16:21.143
the painting to become a painter.

01:16:22.430 --> 01:16:24.820
- Yeah. I mean, he was a man who had

01:16:24.820 --> 01:16:26.343
a lot of reality to give up.

01:16:27.474 --> 01:16:31.518
- Right, and that's also
part of your questions.

01:16:31.518 --> 01:16:35.630
What about the camps and
how did that impact him?

01:16:35.630 --> 01:16:37.580
I think he was determined not to become

01:16:37.580 --> 01:16:40.453
a chronicler in paint of those years.

01:16:40.453 --> 01:16:45.453
And I think he unfortunately
was in a position

01:16:46.390 --> 01:16:49.760
where he had to become
a chronicler in words,

01:16:49.760 --> 01:16:52.590
in literary form, but he was determined

01:16:52.590 --> 01:16:55.450
not to make that the
subject of his paintings.

01:16:55.450 --> 01:16:58.150
And that was part of a
duality that existed in him

01:16:58.150 --> 01:17:00.685
for a long time and it
took him a very long time

01:17:00.685 --> 01:17:05.040
to honor the ability to
paint from within himself,

01:17:05.040 --> 01:17:10.040
somehow not being selfishly self-absorbed,

01:17:10.230 --> 01:17:12.610
which he could not afford to be otherwise.

01:17:12.610 --> 01:17:16.186
- Yeah, and in our class,

01:17:16.186 --> 01:17:19.460
we now have to have some
nice questions from students

01:17:19.460 --> 01:17:21.180
that I'm going to serve
up, but in our class,

01:17:21.180 --> 01:17:23.630
we're reading a bit of
Inhuman Land, as you know,

01:17:25.196 --> 01:17:26.430
and having read the correspondence

01:17:27.638 --> 01:17:29.223
between between him and Hering,

01:17:30.323 --> 01:17:33.603
it's very clear just how much he suffered.

01:17:34.745 --> 01:17:39.745
It was obviously difficult
to return to the period,

01:17:40.111 --> 01:17:43.820
but he wasn't a writer. He
didn't see himself as a writer.

01:17:43.820 --> 01:17:45.060
I mean, he was a wonderful writer,

01:17:45.060 --> 01:17:46.710
but he didn't see himself as a writer.

01:17:46.710 --> 01:17:49.200
It was hard for him. It
was difficult for him.

01:17:49.200 --> 01:17:51.527
He felt that like, as you
suggest, this was the wrong genre.

01:17:51.527 --> 01:17:56.030
Somehow he was in the
wrong artistic orbit.

01:17:56.030 --> 01:17:59.010
This was not what he
was meant to be doing.

01:17:59.010 --> 01:18:04.010
And, you know, the product,
the Inhuman Land book,

01:18:04.344 --> 01:18:06.570
is a result of such struggle,

01:18:06.570 --> 01:18:09.030
not just struggle
because of the substance,

01:18:09.030 --> 01:18:11.390
but also struggled because of the form.

01:18:11.390 --> 01:18:13.530
And that doesn't come through,

01:18:13.530 --> 01:18:18.140
but it even exists as a kind of testimony

01:18:18.140 --> 01:18:20.800
to his determination which also comes out

01:18:20.800 --> 01:18:22.870
in the ability to just keep painting

01:18:22.870 --> 01:18:27.290
decade after decade after decade
after decade after decade.

01:18:27.290 --> 01:18:28.870
I mean, go ahead, sorry.

01:18:28.870 --> 01:18:32.973
- I was going to say he writes
very clearly like a painter.

01:18:34.290 --> 01:18:36.150
That's very good, but he was determined

01:18:36.150 --> 01:18:38.340
not to paint like a writer.

01:18:38.340 --> 01:18:41.343
- Yeah. Yes. That's beautiful.

01:18:42.980 --> 01:18:46.571
Okay. I'm going to give to
the floor now to our students.

01:18:46.571 --> 01:18:51.220
One of our students begins with
actually a classic question

01:18:51.220 --> 01:18:53.580
and one on which my own perspective

01:18:53.580 --> 01:18:55.770
has changed, partly thanks to your work.

01:18:55.770 --> 01:18:57.390
One of our students asked,

01:18:57.390 --> 01:19:01.397
is Czapski remembered as
the historical figure,

01:19:01.397 --> 01:19:04.802
as the chronicler, as the survivor,

01:19:04.802 --> 01:19:08.889
or is he remembered as the artist?

01:19:08.889 --> 01:19:10.900
And I think part of the question is,

01:19:10.900 --> 01:19:13.000
should he remember it
as a historical figure,

01:19:13.000 --> 01:19:15.990
or should he be remembered as the artist?

01:19:15.990 --> 01:19:19.380
- It's a very complicated
question. A very good question.

01:19:19.380 --> 01:19:22.923
It depends upon who's
doing the remembering.

01:19:24.390 --> 01:19:26.760
I think when I first went to Poland

01:19:26.760 --> 01:19:30.503
to begin researching this in 2013,

01:19:34.030 --> 01:19:39.030
people in Poland were not
really aware of Czapski

01:19:39.810 --> 01:19:42.091
as anything but a historical figure.

01:19:42.091 --> 01:19:45.080
He was known as the survivor of Katyn,

01:19:45.080 --> 01:19:47.470
he was the one who carried the torch.

01:19:47.470 --> 01:19:49.979
If anything, he was admired for what

01:19:49.979 --> 01:19:54.979
seemed to be his national pride,

01:19:56.740 --> 01:20:00.450
which was not at all how he
really experienced his life.

01:20:03.274 --> 01:20:07.197
In those years since then,

01:20:07.197 --> 01:20:10.123
I think what I've been able to find

01:20:10.123 --> 01:20:14.450
is a greater interest in
his work as a painter,

01:20:14.450 --> 01:20:19.450
because in Poland, the
standards of visual arts

01:20:22.310 --> 01:20:25.080
are very much controlled by the past,

01:20:25.080 --> 01:20:26.450
by the relation to the past,

01:20:26.450 --> 01:20:29.991
there's an enormously freeing and powerful

01:20:29.991 --> 01:20:34.991
graphic arts part of Polish art world.

01:20:35.620 --> 01:20:39.350
But the painting itself
is very much not unlike

01:20:39.350 --> 01:20:44.350
what Czapski faced when he was
in the academy in the '20s.

01:20:44.650 --> 01:20:46.490
There is this tradition,

01:20:46.490 --> 01:20:49.840
and then there is the kind of fashionable,

01:20:49.840 --> 01:20:53.860
and Czapski seems somehow
now to speak to people

01:20:53.860 --> 01:20:58.110
more directly because
pf his individualism,

01:20:58.110 --> 01:21:00.680
which was considered
a weakness earlier on,

01:21:00.680 --> 01:21:02.700
but it's now considered a strength.

01:21:02.700 --> 01:21:07.150
So I do think that Czapski
is an exceptional painter.

01:21:07.150 --> 01:21:09.870
I'm not making a case for
him being a great painter.

01:21:09.870 --> 01:21:12.570
For me, his life, his devotion

01:21:12.570 --> 01:21:15.050
to painting is really what's key.

01:21:15.050 --> 01:21:20.020
I don't think you can
put him on a platform.

01:21:20.020 --> 01:21:21.899
There are many great painters.

01:21:21.899 --> 01:21:25.825
There's a question here also of market.

01:21:25.825 --> 01:21:29.090
There's no real market value for Czapski.

01:21:29.090 --> 01:21:32.710
So it's very hard to assess
him in terms of his greatness,

01:21:32.710 --> 01:21:35.036
because in the current art world,

01:21:35.036 --> 01:21:38.679
greatness is predicated
upon auction prices,

01:21:38.679 --> 01:21:41.244
and Czapski certainly doesn't appear

01:21:41.244 --> 01:21:45.420
in the stratosphere that way,

01:21:45.420 --> 01:21:48.600
whereas his work represents
a certain accomplishment

01:21:48.600 --> 01:21:53.600
that I find very inspiring
and very meaningful.

01:21:55.080 --> 01:21:59.680
And I think more and more
young people are beginning

01:21:59.680 --> 01:22:02.394
to recognize his work
and gravitate towards it.

01:22:02.394 --> 01:22:07.394
- The next question I would
ask you from a second student,

01:22:07.559 --> 01:22:12.559
I'm just going to ask it in her words

01:22:13.000 --> 01:22:16.110
'cause I think she's onto
something which is very important

01:22:16.110 --> 01:22:19.133
and it compliments what we
were just talking about.

01:22:19.133 --> 01:22:23.460
During his time in imprisonment,

01:22:23.460 --> 01:22:26.163
Czapski experienced
absence in a profound way.

01:22:27.260 --> 01:22:30.860
The men he saw one day were gone the next.

01:22:30.860 --> 01:22:32.740
In what ways do you
think that has exposure

01:22:32.740 --> 01:22:36.692
to systemic loss or removal
informed his artistic style,

01:22:36.692 --> 01:22:40.473
specifically his fixation on the subject?

01:22:43.140 --> 01:22:44.103
- On the subject?

01:22:46.170 --> 01:22:49.434
Well, Czapski's mother
died when he was six

01:22:49.434 --> 01:22:53.660
and the sensation of loss is something

01:22:53.660 --> 01:22:55.843
that he's known his entire life.

01:22:56.924 --> 01:23:00.647
I think that it informs everything

01:23:00.647 --> 01:23:03.650
in an aesthetic sense with him,

01:23:03.650 --> 01:23:06.340
that the idea of absence and presence,

01:23:06.340 --> 01:23:11.340
that dichotomy, that kind of
attempt to show the unseen,

01:23:14.570 --> 01:23:18.050
all of those revolve around this tentative

01:23:18.050 --> 01:23:21.330
relationship to the world around us.

01:23:21.330 --> 01:23:25.603
I think he is profoundly
immersed in a world of loss

01:23:30.440 --> 01:23:35.440
and his role, his life
work, like Ludwig Hering,

01:23:40.070 --> 01:23:43.987
is not to be eclipsed
by that sense of loss,

01:23:43.987 --> 01:23:48.030
not to be crippled by
it, but to understand it

01:23:48.030 --> 01:23:50.383
and to use it as a generative force.

01:23:52.360 --> 01:23:55.945
- Okay, another student asks,

01:23:55.945 --> 01:24:00.450
much of Czapski's art seemed to center

01:24:00.450 --> 01:24:04.163
the act of looking,
especially looking at art.

01:24:05.660 --> 01:24:09.720
Do the body languages and facial
expressions of the figures

01:24:09.720 --> 01:24:13.689
in his paintings convey
anything about his own attitude

01:24:13.689 --> 01:24:17.833
about looking critically
or looking closely?

01:24:19.890 --> 01:24:22.350
- Well, I think that
became a subject for him

01:24:22.350 --> 01:24:24.537
is clear that yes, it
was very important to him

01:24:24.537 --> 01:24:26.260
and it tells a great deal about

01:24:27.149 --> 01:24:28.831
his involvement in that world.

01:24:28.831 --> 01:24:31.773
I think he celebrated reactive looking.

01:24:32.740 --> 01:24:35.620
The monograph that I put
together of his work,

01:24:35.620 --> 01:24:38.130
I call an apprenticeship of looking,

01:24:38.130 --> 01:24:41.970
which was his term for
what he didn't consider

01:24:41.970 --> 01:24:44.330
that he had a career as a painter.

01:24:44.330 --> 01:24:46.540
He never valued himself that highly.

01:24:46.540 --> 01:24:47.570
He always thought of it,

01:24:47.570 --> 01:24:51.150
what he called as an
apprenticeship he had of looking,

01:24:51.150 --> 01:24:54.553
and looking, (indistinct) said,

01:24:59.070 --> 01:25:02.243
is the longest of all the
apprenticeships in the arts.

01:25:03.130 --> 01:25:06.449
And that's really very
true. So this is what he is.

01:25:06.449 --> 01:25:10.177
Again, celebrating this
idea of the engagement

01:25:10.177 --> 01:25:12.980
of a viewer with the work of art.

01:25:12.980 --> 01:25:17.389
And also his is an
opportunity to have that time,

01:25:17.389 --> 01:25:22.389
not just lost in the
contemplation of a work of art,

01:25:23.590 --> 01:25:25.310
he wanted to keep it active.

01:25:25.310 --> 01:25:27.340
So he would step back from the painting

01:25:27.340 --> 01:25:29.210
and see the room that he was in.

01:25:29.210 --> 01:25:32.010
See the gallery, see the
people who were involved, also.

01:25:36.770 --> 01:25:40.410
- Some of the remaining questions
are on overlapping themes

01:25:40.410 --> 01:25:43.058
and some of them, I think we can best

01:25:43.058 --> 01:25:45.780
address in class
tomorrow, and we have made

01:25:45.780 --> 01:25:50.780
you work not for 90 years,
but for at least 90 minutes,

01:25:51.760 --> 01:25:54.950
which is a good chunk of time.

01:25:54.950 --> 01:25:57.119
I want to thank you again, Eric,

01:25:57.119 --> 01:26:00.110
first of all for all of
the work you have done

01:26:00.110 --> 01:26:02.620
to bring Czapski to the world,

01:26:02.620 --> 01:26:06.340
you have begun a renewal,
which I hope will continue.

01:26:06.340 --> 01:26:08.894
And I hope this lecture in this class

01:26:08.894 --> 01:26:10.980
will do something towards bringing his way

01:26:10.980 --> 01:26:13.262
of looking at things that
everyone should care about

01:26:13.262 --> 01:26:16.667
closer to the center of conversations.

01:26:16.667 --> 01:26:18.920
I want to thank you for entertaining

01:26:18.920 --> 01:26:20.830
the questions of our class.

01:26:20.830 --> 01:26:24.038
I want to thank students for
engaging with this difficult

01:26:24.038 --> 01:26:26.880
material after their previous difficult

01:26:26.880 --> 01:26:30.043
material and for being with us.

01:26:30.043 --> 01:26:32.045
And I want to thank all of our guests,

01:26:32.045 --> 01:26:32.878
for our numerous guests,

01:26:33.886 --> 01:26:35.330
for joining us in this Franke lecture.

01:26:35.330 --> 01:26:37.100
So I'm Timothy Snyder.

01:26:37.100 --> 01:26:39.310
We've been listening to Eric Karpeles

01:26:39.310 --> 01:26:43.110
and I'm very pleased, very happy,

01:26:43.110 --> 01:26:45.440
that we could bring all of
this together in the framework

01:26:45.440 --> 01:26:47.671
of the Franke lectures and the framework

01:26:47.671 --> 01:26:49.640
of this class. So thank you again, Eric.

01:26:49.640 --> 01:26:52.190
- Thank you very much to everyone involved

01:26:52.190 --> 01:26:57.190
and I respect and appreciate the work

01:26:59.330 --> 01:27:01.920
that's being done by you and the class

01:27:01.920 --> 01:27:04.070
and the idea of bringing this material

01:27:04.070 --> 01:27:08.240
to a bigger world and a
deeper understanding of it.

01:27:08.240 --> 01:27:09.553
So thank you very much.

